WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:07.883
- Very excited to welcome you

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:11.610
to the Third Annual Seong-Yawng
Park Memorial Lecture,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.650
Seong-Yawng Park, Graduate School of 1965,

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.420
and Marguerite Clark
Park Memorial Lecture.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.180
We were really just getting
started with the Park Lecture

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.120
a few years ago, and then COVID happened.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.410
So although the lecture's been
around for about five years,

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.880
this is only the third one,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.520
and I will let our chair, Hwansoo Kim,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.380
introduce our speaker.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.070
But just briefly, I'd like
to tell you a little bit

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.960
about the lecture itself.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.160
The Seong-Yawng Park and
Marguerite Clark Park Lecture

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.310
was made possible by
a really generous gift

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.060
from the Estate of the Park family.

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.000
And so the principal objective of it

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.630
is to foster the study of Korea at Yale

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.720
by bringing recognition
to the Korean Peninsula

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.460
and its affairs and achievements

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.090
to Yale and the wider community.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.060
And, you know, I'd like
to just briefly say

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.060
that Prof. Dr. Park was a really generous

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.770
and insightful alumni, alum for Yale.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:00.963
He graduated from,

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.280
he earned a PhD here in 1965 in Economics,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.560
and then he went on to teach

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.430
at the University of
California, Berkeley school

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.750
before expanding his father's company

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.350
into one of the top
conglomerates in Korea.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.313
But importantly for Yale,
he really had a very,

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.090
a really active role in
promoting Yale in Korea, right?

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.820
And as we work now to
build our Korea program,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:28.120
that's really an honor to be
able to continue that legacy.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.640
So among the other things
that he did at Yale,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.473
he was a member

00:01:30.473 --> 00:01:32.810
of President's Council on
International Activities.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.190
He was on the Board of Advisors

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.870
for the Yale Economic Growth Center,

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.400
and served for many years as the president

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.890
of the Yale Club of Korea.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.940
And so, in many ways he was really

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.200
Yale's biggest champion in Korea,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.100
and we're really so glad to be
able to continue that legacy.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.750
But with that, I'll turn things
over to Prof. Hwansoo Kim,

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:52.620
Professor of Religious Studies,

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.170
to introduce our speaker today.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.360
- Thank you so much for coming

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.003
on a very chilly day. (laughs)

00:02:10.279 --> 00:02:12.910
So it's my tremendous honor and pleasure

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:16.963
to able to introduce my senior
colleague, Prof. Jin Park.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.050
Dr. Park is currently Professor

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.340
and the Chair of the Department
of Philosophy and Religion

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.870
at American University.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:27.750
She received her B.A. and M.A.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.960
from Yonsei University
in Seoul, South Korea,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.470
and her PhD from the State
University of New York

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.003
at Stony Brook in 1998.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.760
Prof. Park is not only a prominent scholar

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.390
of Korean Buddhist Studies,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:49.390
but also a leading scholar of
Korean Studies in the West.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.050
It's true that Prof.
Robert Buswell (chuckles)

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.650
has been the towering
figure of Korean Buddhism,

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.610
but he's retiring this
semester, (chuckles)

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:02.710
so while Prof. Park is nowhere
near retirement herself,

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:07.080
and while she might
disagree with me on this,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.180
but I would say that
very soon she will become

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.253
the most senior scholar of our field.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:17.150
Likewise, in terms of
Korean Studies broadly,

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:21.090
she's now one of the most
accomplished female scholars

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.180
in the field as well.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.500
So for me, personally,
for the past 15 years,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.060
Prof. Park has been a close
intellectual interlocutor,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.350
colleague and mentor,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.923
deeply influencing and guiding
my research in many ways.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.880
Her interdisciplinary
and versatile research

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.853
explores Korean and East Asian Buddhism,

00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:47.470
intercultural philosophy,
modern Korean philosophy,

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.270
and intercultural ethics,

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:53.180
focusing on the intersections
of gender, violence,

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.180
the politics of discrimination
and marginalization,

00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:59.863
and narrative identity.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:06.050
She is a very prolific writer,
having published three books,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.170
six edited and co-edited volumes,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:14.960
and more than 60 articles
and book chapters so far.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.860
So in the first book,

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.920
titled "Buddhism and
Postmodernism: Zen, Huayan,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.620
and the Possibility of
Buddhist Postmodern Ethics,"

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.940
published in 2008,

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.940
Prof. Park dispels century-old
suspicions of Buddhism

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.720
as a religion of nothingness

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.393
or one that disregard philosophers.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.240
And she offers the ethics of tension

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.480
as a potential ethical paradigm

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.410
drawn from Zen and Huayan Buddhism

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.460
and postmodern philosophy,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.943
with a special focus on Jacques
Derrida's deconstruction.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:57.430
The next book, titled "Reflections
of a Zen Buddhist Nun,"

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:02.140
published in 2014, is a
meticulous translation

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:06.220
of a bestseller book
published in Korean in 1960

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:11.220
by Kim Iryŏp, a writer,
first-generation Korean feminist,

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.660
Buddhist nun, and philosopher,
as you can see on the screen,

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.930
who presents a creative interpretation

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.370
of Buddhist philosophy and practice.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.570
The third one, "Women
and Buddhist Philosophy:

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.330
Engaging Zen Master Kim Iryŏp,"

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:30.260
is a sequel to "Reflections
of a Zen Buddhist Nun,"

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.613
but a broader, in-depth
analysis of Kim Iryŏp's oeuvre.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:38.660
Prof. Park proposes a new
mode of philosophizing

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.100
based on the discussion

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.123
of Kim Iryŏp's life
experience and worldview.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.700
She terms this new mode of philosophizing

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.020
as narrative philosophy,
which engages itself

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.510
with a narrative discourse
of our daily experiences

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:55.920
instead of relying heavily

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.853
on theorization and abstraction.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:02.140
So I'm sure Prof. Park
will elaborate more on this

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.163
in today's talk.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.320
As I said, Prof. Park is
also editor and co-editor

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.247
of five volumes:

00:06:10.247 --> 00:06:15.017
"Approaches to Chan, Sŏn,
Zen Studies," in 2022,

00:06:15.017 --> 00:06:18.173
"Makers of Modern Korean Buddhism," 2010,

00:06:19.427 --> 00:06:23.920
"Merleau-Ponty and Buddhism," 2010,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.237
and also "Buddhism and
Deconstructions," 2006,

00:06:27.237 --> 00:06:29.000
"Comparative Political Theory

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.000
and Cross-Cultural Philosophy," in 2009.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.700
And in fact, she and I
have recently finished

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.470
another co-edited volume,

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:42.850
called "New Perspectives
in Modern Korean Buddhism:

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.570
Institution, Gender and Secular Society,"

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:48.683
which will come out by SUNY this December.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.760
Prof. Park is a recipient
of many numerous grants

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.060
and has taught as a visiting professor

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.313
in Korea, Japan, Canada, and Germany.

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:06.530
She's equally active in
professional leadership and service.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.880
She currently serves as the Vice President

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.830
of the American Academy of Religion.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.750
In fact, she's the first East Asianist

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.440
to be elected to the position

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.913
in the Academy's 100-year history.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.740
She's also the president

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:25.290
of the North American Korean
Philosophy Association,

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.670
and also the president

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.503
of the Society for Asian
and Comparative Philosophy.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.510
So it's so great to have you over here.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:34.410
Without further ado,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.041
please join me in
welcoming Prof. Jin Park.

00:07:37.041 --> 00:07:38.876
(audience applauding)

00:07:38.876 --> 00:07:42.507
- Thank you.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:46.060
Okay, can you hear me? Good.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.850
Thank you very much for
the generous introduction

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:50.630
and thank you for having me here.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.470
I'm really happy to be here

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:56.680
and sharing my research
with the Yale community.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.180
So after the pandemic,

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.640
even though we are still wearing masks,

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.950
just, in-person event is both exciting

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:05.710
and a little bit worrisome, right?

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.650
So I know.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:12.170
So in the department, we just
did the first in-person event

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:14.620
since the pandemic, a couple of weeks ago.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.280
It's exciting, but at the same
time, something is different.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.160
So, but anyway, I'm really
glad to be here in person,

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.423
not on Zoom.

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:27.060
And I'd like to share
with you about my research

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.260
on women and then women and Buddhism.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.300
So as Prof. Kim mentioned,

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.130
this is research that
I did for a long time,

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.170
and a little bit of a
genesis of this research.

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:41.350
So after I finished my dissertation

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.113
on Buddhism and postmodernism,

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:47.330
my focus at that time, in
Buddhism, was premodern.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.040
I was working on

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.000
a 13th century Korean Sŏn master, Chinul.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.920
So after I finished my dissertation,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.200
I began to think about
modern Korean Buddhism.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:01.340
What happened in Buddhism in modern time?

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:06.340
Because after all, I compared
13th century Sŏn Buddhism

00:09:06.383 --> 00:09:08.690
with 20th century French philosophy.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.610
So what happened to Buddhism nowadays?

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:13.153
So I began my research on
modern Korean Buddhism and,

00:09:13.153 --> 00:09:15.680
believe it or not, about 20 years ago,

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.470
the modern Korean Buddhism was
not studied, even in Korea.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:21.080
It was a very rare occasion.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.980
It changed rapidly for the past 20 years.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:27.510
But anyway, as I started working
on modern Korean Buddhism,

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:31.220
naturally a question arose:
what happened to women?

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.083
Where are they?

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.370
The available materials are all
about Buddhist monks, right,

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:40.760
or man, obviously, as usual.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:45.040
And I couldn't find the
materials on Korean nuns.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:49.630
In terms of number, actually,

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:51.780
there are a little bit
more Korean Buddhist nuns

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:56.640
than monks in Korea, but
then we don't have materials.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.490
So after kind of struggling
to find the materials,

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:04.140
I came across a book, which
is not an academic book,

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.120
but a book written by a journalist.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:10.160
He now got his PhD and
he became an academic,

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.750
but he was a reporter
in a Buddhist newspaper,

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.810
and he was interested
in, for whatever reason,

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.300
interested in Buddhist nuns.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.873
So he kind of began to run
a series on a newspaper,

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:27.200
introducing Korean Buddhist
nuns in modern period,

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:31.240
starting from the very beginning,
which was in 1910, 1920.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:35.520
So each chapter in the book
has about 10 pages, 15 pages,

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:36.380
about the life,

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.820
and then when the person
became a Buddhist nun,

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.270
what the person did, and died, right?

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:46.320
And when I got to Kim Iryŏp's
section, that's only 15 pages.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.970
I read it and thought, "Wow,
this figure is interesting.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:51.830
There might be something."

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.570
But because it's only 15 pages,

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:55.790
I couldn't kind of tell much about it.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.120
So there begin the journey,
or there began the journey.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:01.793
So I began to look up the materials.

00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:06.110
I've been to all the kind
of secondhand bookstores

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:09.453
and then library book copies,
and so on and so forth.

00:11:11.373 --> 00:11:14.030
And it was an interesting kind of journey.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:17.050
So as I just told you, that I encounter,

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:21.120
I discovered Kim Iryŏp
as a Buddhist scholar.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.750
But once I began to talk about Kim Iryŏp,

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:28.100
my parents' generation,
they all know Kim Iryŏp.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.050
Not because they are Buddhist scholar.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:34.520
Because Kim Iryŏp was a
celebrity in her time, right?

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.240
So that really gave me
also another thought.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:41.600
If she was that much influential
for my parents' generation,

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.210
how come I don't know her,

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.060
even though I'm a Buddhist scholar?

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:48.540
So a lot of questions
kind of came to my mind,

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.660
and over the almost two decades,

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.770
my questions about Kim
Iryŏp, Kim Iryŏp's works

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:56.920
changes and edited,

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.380
so there are kind of a lot of
different kind of questions

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:03.121
and issues I explored with Kim Iryŏp.

00:12:03.121 --> 00:12:03.990
And that was also

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:07.110
because Kim Iryŏp's life
itself is panoramic.

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.770
There are different dimensions in her life

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:14.200
and each stage kind of gave
us new kind of questions.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:18.770
So in this presentation,
I will give you kind of a,

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:20.340
I will tell you about Kim Iryŏp's life

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.540
from the beginning
almost to the end, right?

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:27.540
And that story I'd like to
give you within the big frame.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.300
So these are two questions
I want to kind of ask

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:32.610
in this presentation.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:35.530
First one has to do
with women and Buddhism.

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:38.563
So, how and why do women
engage with Buddhism?

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:42.170
So I mean, each individual
has a different reason

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:45.690
to learn Buddhism or practice Buddhism

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.370
and so on and so forth.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.560
Now, my question here is that,

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.990
whether women has specific
reasons or journey

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:57.903
which is different from
their male counterpart.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.300
That's one question, which
is related to, obviously,

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.570
gender issue, and women's issue,

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:05.730
and Buddhism and gender issue.

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.870
And I'd like to expand the
scope a little bit more.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.320
And the second question then,

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.270
women in modern Korean
intellectual history.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:16.240
As you will see shortly,
definitely Kim Iryŏp

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:20.030
was one of the main figure,
from my perspective,

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.670
in modern Korean intellectual history.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:27.140
But where is she in the history

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:29.563
of modern intellectual kind of history?

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.730
So the second question would be,

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.740
women in modern Korean
intellectual history,

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.143
how and where do they meet?

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:40.980
So I'd like to address these questions

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:44.219
by going through Kim
Iryŏp's life and thought.

00:13:44.219 --> 00:13:48.660
Kim Iryŏp was born 1896 and died 1971,

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.060
and obviously, Korean Buddhist nun-thinker

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:55.300
So there are different kinds
of phases in Kim Iryŏp life,

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:57.230
which kind of shows us

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:00.470
different intellectual
aspects of Kim Iryŏp.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:03.423
So the first one is her
Christian background.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:09.530
Kim Iryŏp's father was a Christian pastor.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:12.790
And look at the date. She was born 1896.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:15.430
So her father was a first-generation

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:18.210
Korean Protestant pastor, right?

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.790
And obviously, he was very faithful.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:25.440
Kim Iryŏp describe her
father, in one of her essay,

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.720
that he is most faithful Christian

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:31.580
in the entire Korea, right?

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:36.580
And her mother, obviously, was
also very faithful Christian.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.550
Growing up in this kind
of a Christian family,

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:44.510
Kim Iryŏp herself was obviously
very faithful Christian.

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:49.250
And she envisioned her future
as a Christian missionary

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:51.600
when she was only seven
or eight years old.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.200
She said that she was gonna go to a place

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.900
where the people do not know about God.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.543
She's gonna send God's message.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:00.630
Well, that was good.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:03.970
But then eventually,
during her teenage years,

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.910
she began to have doubts
about Christianity.

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:09.460
And I think these doubts she had

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:13.190
were kind of doubts any
Christian could have had.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:15.850
So the questions like,
if God created the world,

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:18.510
why are there so many
bad things in the world,

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.250
and so on and so forth, right?

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.640
These are the questions that theologians

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:24.403
discussed for centuries.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:28.973
But when Kim Iryŏp tried to ask
this question to her father,

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:35.550
who was a very conservative evangelist,

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.477
he stopped her and told her,

00:15:38.477 --> 00:15:40.360
"You think about that kind of thing

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.820
because your faith is not strong enough.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.847
Pray. That is the only answer."

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:49.020
So this kind of attitude
of her father backfired.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.850
So because she could not get
an answer to her questions,

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.120
eventually she lost her
faith in Christianity

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.283
around, she was late-teenage years.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:02.270
Then after that, until
she encountered Buddhism,

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:06.100
then for 10 years, she didn't
have a religion, right?

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:09.660
Another kind of important
aspect of Kim Iryŏp's life

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.083
is her education.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:17.210
So Kim Iryŏp received
education all the way up

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:20.657
to what now is called college.

00:16:20.657 --> 00:16:23.780
Ewha Haktang, which opened in 1886,

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:27.710
was the first women's higher education,

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:30.570
established by a Christian
missionary, obviously.

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:31.650
But think about this.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.980
The beginning of the 20th
century, in Confucian Korea,

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.810
women were not supposed to get educated.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:40.380
Even when the opportunity
opened like this,

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:42.870
the first year that Ewha Haktang opened,

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:44.730
there was no student,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:48.060
and then there came one student
who actually, eventually,

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:51.050
became a translator for the Royal family.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:53.253
So in Confucian Korea,

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:58.710
educating women is a bad idea, right,

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:01.090
because women does not need an education

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:05.100
to be a good wife and mother.

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:08.170
But Kim Iryŏp was one
of this first generation

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:13.170
of Korean women who
received public education,

00:17:13.340 --> 00:17:16.010
Western-style education, for women.

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:19.360
And Kim Iryŏp credits this
kind of whole opportunity

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:23.270
to get educated, to her
parents' Christianity.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:24.450
I think that's true.

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.770
Because her parents kind
of were faithful Christian,

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:30.260
they learned more about
the Western culture

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:33.290
where women's education studied earlier.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:36.087
So her mother told her that,

00:17:36.087 --> 00:17:37.370
"Even though you are a daughter,

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:39.480
I will educate you like any sons."

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:43.127
And her mother was criticized
by the village people.

00:17:43.127 --> 00:17:45.240
"Why are you educating a girl?

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:46.870
What's good about doing that?"

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:49.480
But she did get education.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.920
And after she finished at Ewha Hakdang,

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:56.840
she also had an opportunity
to study in Japan

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.900
from 1919 to 1920.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:02.550
As many Koreans nowadays come to U.S.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:05.060
to get an advanced education,

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:09.320
during the early 20th century,
many Koreans went to Japan

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:12.060
to receive advanced kind of education,

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.080
and Kim Iryŏp went to Japan.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.430
And I believe it was in Japan

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:21.120
she really got this idea
about the women's movement.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:26.070
So as soon as she came
back from Japan, 1920,

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:31.013
she kind of established with
other, kind of her friends,

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:36.343
the journal titled
"Sinyŏja," meaning New Woman.

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:39.850
She became the editor-in-chief,

00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:42.040
and this journal is credited

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.020
as the first one published by women

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:46.650
for the benefit of women.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.123
So that's a big thing. So that's 1920.

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:53.360
And she was the editor-in-chief,
which is big thing, right?

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:58.030
And here you clearly see
the kind of influence

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:00.413
of Japanese feminist activists.

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:04.070
Because if you know the Japanese case,

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:08.330
then in Japan, early 1910, 1911,

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:12.760
there were a group of female intellectuals

00:19:12.760 --> 00:19:15.050
who were very much actively involved

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:16.270
with the women's movement.

00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:19.410
And Hiratsuka Raichō
was the leading figure

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:24.314
who created the journal called "Seitō."

00:19:24.314 --> 00:19:26.701
Seitō literally means Bluestockings.

00:19:26.701 --> 00:19:27.907
You know, the Bluestockings, right,

00:19:27.907 --> 00:19:30.470
which in kind of 18th century in Europe

00:19:30.470 --> 00:19:33.810
then meant a kind of
radical kind of woman.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:34.643
Well, at the beginning,

00:19:34.643 --> 00:19:37.460
both men and women were bluestockings.

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:41.043
So literally, it means a
kind of women's movement.

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:44.290
When Kim Iryŏp came back to Korea,

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:47.060
not only she created journal "Sinyŏja,"

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:49.870
but she also created a forum

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:51.920
in which women get all together,

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:53.773
discuss about women's issue,

00:19:53.773 --> 00:19:57.980
with the name of which
the group was Ch'ŏngt'ap.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:01.577
Now, the Chinese and
Korean, Korean and Japanese,

00:20:01.577 --> 00:20:03.240
(speaking in foreign language)

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.370
has a different pronunciation.

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:10.230
So "Seitō" in Korean
pronunciation is Ch'ŏngt'ap.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:13.290
So Kim Iryŏp definitely
used that expression.

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:14.800
But interestingly, Kim Iryŏp,

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:17.560
I don't know whether she was
not aware of this expression,

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:20.950
bluestockings, or she
didn't like bluestockings,

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.180
so she changed the second character,

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:26.450
so meaning of her forum became Blue Tower

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:28.090
instead of Bluestockings.

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:31.720
But here we clearly see that
the Japanese new women's

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:33.343
influence on Kim Iryŏp.

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:39.380
So if you see her life in
the 1920s, from 1920 to 1928,

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:43.320
Iryŏp was a very engaged writer,
women's movement activist,

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:44.760
and a public intellectual.

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:49.350
Here you see Kim Iryŏp at the
center with Korean writers.

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:53.357
When I show this to my
students, I ask them,

00:20:53.357 --> 00:20:54.190
"Who do you think is Kim Iryŏp?"

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:55.957
And students always say, "Center," right?

00:20:55.957 --> 00:20:56.991
"Center is Kim Iryŏp."

00:20:56.991 --> 00:20:59.010
(chuckles) I don't know how
they kind of notice that,

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.860
but the woman with
glasses, that's Kim Iryŏp.

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:03.207
And then also here,

00:21:03.207 --> 00:21:06.217
Kim Iryŏp was giving a
public lecture in 1962.

00:21:06.217 --> 00:21:09.170
Prof. Kim kind of showed me this picture,

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:10.450
led me to this picture.

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:13.910
So this is something, that
woman gave a public lecture

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:15.793
in Korea in 1920s.

00:21:17.884 --> 00:21:18.717
Now,

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:23.770
in the inaugural,

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:26.660
the kind of first issue of "New Women,"

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:29.250
this is kind of what we read.

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:32.350
So what are their demands?

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:33.693
So here is the first:

00:21:33.693 --> 00:21:36.720
economic independence, obviously, right?

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:40.900
Second, rationalization and
simplification of family system,

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:44.290
because family life is a
kind of core of women's life.

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:47.933
Third, rejection of a
male-dominated traditional thought.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:51.270
Fourth, a call for the stronger awareness

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:53.493
of women's responsibility and duties.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:57.460
The statement is a little bit ambiguous.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:00.280
So right, what are the
responsibilities and duties?

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:03.860
Fifth, campaigns by women's organizations

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:06.230
and female students for Old Women,

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:09.670
so that they could get aware
of various women's issues,

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:12.170
including health and child-education.

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:14.510
So here, it could be a little bit radical,

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:16.580
but it's not really radical-radical

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:20.473
in terms of demands of women
for their freedom, right?

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:24.210
Well, what Kim Iryŏp

00:22:26.046 --> 00:22:28.710
became criticized by the society

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:32.980
and under notoriety, was
through her challenging

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:36.523
about the body politics of Korean society.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:38.970
So Kim Iryŏp said

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:42.900
that society's control
of feminine sexuality

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:47.270
in the name of virginity and
chastity is a visible form

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.640
of gender discrimination
in Korean society,

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:53.540
and Kim Iryŏp demanded sexual
freedom, freedom to love,

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:54.940
and freedom to get divorced.

00:22:56.860 --> 00:22:58.720
So here you begin to see that,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:03.240
how Korean society in 1920s,
within the Confucian tradition,

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.053
would accept this kind of demand.

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:11.127
When I first read this demand
of Kim Iryŏp, I thought,

00:23:11.127 --> 00:23:13.690
"Wow, how was she able to be

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:17.340
this much radical in 1920s Korea?"

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:22.340
I mean, even the end of 20th
century, early 21st century,

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:25.830
in Korea, this could be a radical demand.

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:27.380
And then I learned that, well,

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.700
this is not only Kim Iryŏp's idea,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.420
but this was a kind of general trend

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.150
in the New Women movement,

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:39.150
which was based on the
Swedish thinker, Ellen Key,

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:40.330
but I'm not gonna go there.

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:44.650
But anyway, so this
was Kim Iryŏp's demand,

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:47.303
known as "New Theory of
Chastity," (speaks in Korean).

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:51.210
But what she said

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:54.900
is not that we should get rid of chastity.

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:56.720
That's not what Kim Iryŏp said, right?

00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:59.230
Chastity is obviously

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:03.070
a visible form of gender
discrimination in Korean society,

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:06.020
but she's not saying that
therefore we should get rid of it.

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:10.760
But she redefined the meaning of chastity.

00:24:10.760 --> 00:24:15.760
Now, what she says, is that
chastity should be defined

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:19.313
as a maximum state of
love for one's lover,

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:25.330
not a moral rule that
society impose on individual.

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:28.820
And I think it makes good sense,
because what she claim is,

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:33.290
that if somebody is in
love with someone else,

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:36.640
naturally, the person
will maintain chastity,

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:39.520
be faithful to the
person, even when society

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:42.230
doesn't tell the person,
"You should be faithful."

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:44.069
When the love is not strong enough,

00:24:44.069 --> 00:24:45.700
then there comes a problem.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:49.000
So it's not an issue that
society control people

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.233
by making a rule.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.330
And Kim Iryŏp thought
that this kind of idea

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:59.320
is not only for women,
but for men as well.

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:01.560
So here I'm quoting a passage

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:04.290
from "My View on Chastity" article.

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:07.280
Quote, "We, new women and new men,"

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:09.917
here you see that she is
addressing both men and women,

00:25:09.917 --> 00:25:12.420
"who want to do away
with all the conventions,

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:14.730
traditions, concepts,
and who are determined

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:18.080
to bring attention to a new
and fresh concept of life,

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:20.570
cannot but strongly
resist, among other things,

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:22.570
the traditional morality on sex,

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:24.440
which has ignored our personalities

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:27.367
as well as our individual
characteristics."

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:30.840
So here you clearly see already

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:35.290
Kim Iryŏp's desire to really
maintain her personal space,

00:25:35.290 --> 00:25:39.540
which is free from social constraints.

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:43.820
Well, would the Korean society allow that?

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:45.440
That's a really big question.

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:49.730
So Kim Iryŏp was part of
a women's movement group,

00:25:49.730 --> 00:25:51.240
known as New Women,

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:54.060
and New Women was not
just a Korean phenomenon.

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:57.760
It's a global phenomenon
you see in the U.S., 1890s,

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:00.830
in Japan, 1910s, and Korea, 1920s.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:03.060
And in the Korean context,

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:06.330
they usually have several
different types of new women.

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:09.630
And one of the first type
is a liberalist new woman,

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.530
to which Kim Iryŏp belong.

00:26:11.530 --> 00:26:13.150
So their demand is really,

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:15.540
has a lot to do with sexual freedom, okay?

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:17.450
So liberalist new women,

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:21.500
they demanded individual
freedom and freedom to love

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:23.437
and freedom to get divorced
(speaking in Korean).

00:26:26.312 --> 00:26:29.500
So there are three major
figures in this context:

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:33.140
Kim Iryŏp, and Na Hyesŏk,

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:36.570
who was a good friend of Kim Iryŏp,

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:39.660
who worked together with
Kim Iryŏp in women's issues,

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:42.640
who was known as the first female painter

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.930
in modern painting in Korea.

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:47.420
She was a really promising painter.

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:50.570
So I gave one, the
"Self Portrait" in 1928.

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:53.280
Perhaps you notice a certain
kind of very fauvistic,

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:56.610
impressionist, I think, I don't know.

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:58.970
She studied in Japan early on,

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:00.750
from a very wealthy family.

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:03.530
But eventually, she had
a problem with her family

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:06.897
and her marriage and
she died on the street.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:10.240
And another figure is Kim Myŏngsun,

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:12.770
who was also known as
the first female poet

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:14.300
in Western-style poetry,

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:16.775
the first female writer in modern Korea,

00:27:16.775 --> 00:27:18.520
and an actor and journalist.

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:21.197
So these women all had this,

00:27:21.197 --> 00:27:24.680
"The first Korean woman
to this and to do that."

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:27.660
But Kim Myŏngsun also died.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:31.260
She had a mental problem
later and died on the street.

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:34.840
So you see that their
ending was not happy ending,

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:37.280
if you just look at the ending itself.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:42.220
But that's also the issue
that we need to reconsider.

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:43.470
So what happens here?

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:47.730
Kim Iryŏp's challenge to society
in terms of sexual freedom,

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:49.840
and Na Hyesŏk and Kim Myŏngsun,

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:51.670
these new Korean new women,

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:54.550
first generation of Korean feminists,

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:59.360
this is exactly the case
of value collision, right?

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:02.410
Two different values collide each other.

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:06.950
For the New Women, morality
that subjugates women to men

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:08.380
and limits women's life

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:11.913
to that of a wife and mother, is immoral.

00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:15.850
And there is a journal in
which a New Woman says this.

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:17.230
Social morality is immoral

00:28:17.230 --> 00:28:19.350
because the social morality impose women

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:23.363
to do only some thing and
then limits women's freedom.

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:27.000
However, from the society's point of view,

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:28.810
the new women who challenged

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:32.260
the traditional virtue of
women and morality for women,

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:33.430
assigned by the society,

00:28:33.430 --> 00:28:35.130
is immoral and they are destructive.

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:36.080
They are dangerous.

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:40.410
So they are dangerous
women. They are bad women.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:43.900
So until very recently, until
the end of 20th century,

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:45.410
these liberalist New Women,

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:47.520
they were called by all bad names.

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:50.810
Some people even called them
"high society call girl,"

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:55.363
because they talk about
sexuality in public space.

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:02.240
And people, even nowadays, say
that their lives are failure.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:05.320
They failed. Why?

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:08.540
Because Na Hyesŏk's life was a failure

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:12.130
because she divorced and
died alone on the street.

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:13.630
Kim Myŏngsun's life was a failure,

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:16.880
since she failed with her love affairs

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:19.300
and died in a mental hospital.

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:21.080
And Kim Iryŏp's life was a failure,

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:23.500
since she failed in her
marriage and love affairs

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:24.820
and joined the monastery.

00:29:24.820 --> 00:29:26.090
And when I read this passage,

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:28.930
always I find this is really
strange, right? (chuckles)

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:33.683
So joining the monastery is
like dying on the street.

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:37.950
So in other words, the
lives and even the deaths

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:41.040
of the New Women were judged
by the patriarchal ideology.

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:43.850
Not only their lives,
but even their death.

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:48.670
Here, I really want to ask,
what does it mean when we say

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:51.023
that somebody's life's a failure?

00:29:52.030 --> 00:29:54.300
What are the standard
measurements for that?

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:57.670
Who make the standards?
Who make the judgment?

00:29:57.670 --> 00:30:02.290
Can someone's life be judged
by just looking at the end

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:04.270
or one aspect of it?

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:07.240
We all know that our lives are messy.

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:08.677
There're all kind of
elements in there, right?

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:14.200
So I think this is very
important kind of issues

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:15.280
we need to think about,

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:18.603
which also apply to our society right now.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:22.500
The evaluation of the New
Women's lives and deaths

00:30:22.500 --> 00:30:26.200
by their society evidence
that values of one's life

00:30:26.200 --> 00:30:27.110
and one's identity

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:30.100
was strictly controlled
by mainstream discourse.

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:32.580
The mainstream discourse
of Korean society says

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:35.200
that women's lives should be like this:

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:37.180
you should get married,
you should be a wife,

00:30:37.180 --> 00:30:39.700
you should be a mother, and die.

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:41.910
Is that success?
(audience chuckling)

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:44.303
If not, you are a problem, right?

00:30:45.765 --> 00:30:47.220
Individuals in such a society

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:51.300
do not have much room to
creatively engage with their lives.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:53.633
There are different lives possible, right?

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:56.740
So Kim Iryŏp became keenly aware

00:30:56.740 --> 00:31:00.180
of the problems of leading
a life in such a condition.

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:03.380
So during the 1920s, it looks like

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:06.860
Kim Iryŏp was thinking that,
"I can change society."

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:08.687
That's what women activists did, right?

00:31:08.687 --> 00:31:11.160
"I can change society, I
can change social norms,

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:14.870
and then I can free myself
from this patriarchal society."

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:17.910
And eventually she seems to realize that

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:21.890
that's not exactly the end,
that wouldn't be sufficient.

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:25.720
There must be something deeper
than social constraints,

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:30.450
and that's the moment Kim Iryŏp
turns from social activist

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:32.963
to religious practitioner.

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:37.310
So around 1928,

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:40.910
Kim Iryŏp began to practice
the Buddhist meditation.

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:42.850
As I told you before, Kim
Iryŏp was a Christian.

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:45.180
She never knew anything about Buddhism.

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:47.200
She never practiced meditation.

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:51.000
But then suddenly, she kind
of gradually moved there.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:54.620
In 1933, she joined the
monastery, became a Buddhist nun.

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.070
When she joined the monastery,

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:59.450
obviously Korean society
wouldn't trust her,

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:01.520
and said, "Well, she escaped to monastery.

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:03.070
She will come back soon."

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:03.950
No, she didn't.

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:04.870
Right? She didn't.

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:06.550
And then she was a major figure

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:10.223
in Korean Buddhist nuns'
community until her death in 1971.

00:32:11.780 --> 00:32:15.310
So some people say that Kim
Iryŏp joined the monastery

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:17.910
because she failed in her love affairs.

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:20.720
And I say, "Well, if
failing in love affairs

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:22.320
means that you have to go to monastery,

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:24.334
almost everybody should have
joined the monastery, right?"

00:32:24.334 --> 00:32:25.167
(audience chuckles)

00:32:25.167 --> 00:32:27.900
How many people doesn't have
that kind of experience?

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:31.960
So looking at (indistinct)
kind of presentation

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:34.620
about Kim Iryŏp's life, you could see that

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:38.390
there seems to be two distinctive phases

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:39.890
in Kim Iryŏp's life,

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:42.710
and scholars have been claiming that.

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:46.473
In other words, there is a
rupture in Kim Iryŏp's life,

00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:49.513
and the rupture happens, her (indistinct),

00:32:51.566 --> 00:32:52.990
her joining the monastery.

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:54.630
So before she joined the monastery,

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:58.050
she was a feminist
activist, public figure,

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.930
who demanded sexual freedom.

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:05.070
And after she joined the
monastery, she was a Sŏn Zen master

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:07.810
who led the frugal life of a monastic.

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:10.820
I mean, monastery in Korea, it should be,

00:33:10.820 --> 00:33:11.760
yeah, should be poor.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:15.200
I mean, basically monastics
are backwards, traditionally.

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:20.200
In 1930s, '40s, '50s, Korean
monastery was, it looks like

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:22.483
really, really kind of
poor at that time, right?

00:33:22.483 --> 00:33:24.353
And Kim Iryŏp led that life.

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:27.040
But from the very beginning,

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:30.457
I claim that that two
phases of Kim Iryŏp life,

00:33:30.457 --> 00:33:34.493
Kim Iryŏp as a feminist activist,
Kim Iryŏp as a Sŏn Master,

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:37.690
they are not totally two different,

00:33:37.690 --> 00:33:40.830
separate kind of aspects
of Kim Iryŏp's life.

00:33:40.830 --> 00:33:42.870
These two aspects of Kim Iryŏp's life

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:47.910
has kind of one thread, continuing theme,

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:51.330
which is a search for freedom.

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:54.380
She wanted to be free from
the patriarchal society,

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:57.623
and then she wanted to
be free from even that,

00:33:57.623 --> 00:34:02.520
there's something, the
existential kind of binding

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:05.580
as human beings, and by finding freedom,

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:09.560
she wants to be an authentic human being.

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:10.923
And that's my claim.

00:34:11.890 --> 00:34:16.790
And so in looking at Kim
Iryŏp's Buddhism now,

00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:18.070
the first question one can ask

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:20.803
is the relationship between
Buddhism and feminism.

00:34:21.904 --> 00:34:24.170
In American Buddhist scholarship as well,

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:28.300
this issue of relationship
between women and Buddhism,

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:30.130
or Buddhism and feminism,

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:32.870
have been kind of a hot topic for a while.

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:37.840
And some people claim
that Buddhism and feminism

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:39.320
are not compatible.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:40.270
Why?

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:45.040
Because in Buddhism, nothing
has permanent essence.

00:34:45.040 --> 00:34:47.963
Everything exists through
causes and conditions.

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:52.750
Therefore Buddhist concept
of self is no-self.

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:55.600
In other words, there is no
permanent, independent self

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:56.520
in an individual.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:58.810
What we call "self" is combination

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:00.400
of all these causes and conditions.

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:02.750
Therefore it is called a no-self.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:07.060
On the other hand, this claims,

00:35:07.060 --> 00:35:12.060
well, women in a patriarchal
society do not have identity.

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:15.200
When Buddhism claim
no-self, that doesn't help,

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:17.783
because women need to
find the self to lose it.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:21.670
Against this kind of a claim,

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:26.120
Kim Iryŏp definitely demonstrate
Buddhism can help women

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:28.570
to liberate themselves.

00:35:28.570 --> 00:35:32.720
And that's one of the ways of
reading Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism.

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:33.653
So how?

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:41.210
One of the ways Kim Iryŏp
kind of used Buddhism,

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:45.170
Buddhist concept of self,
for her feminists agenda

00:35:45.170 --> 00:35:48.173
is to reinterpret the
concept of Buddhist no-self.

00:35:49.060 --> 00:35:52.690
Kim Iryŏp introduce two types of self.

00:35:52.690 --> 00:35:55.400
One is called small-self, soa.

00:35:55.400 --> 00:35:59.040
The other is called
great-self, or big-self, tae'a.

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:01.940
In other words, the kind of everyday self,

00:36:01.940 --> 00:36:04.880
which is gendered and
which confines individuals

00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:09.010
within their boundaries,
is a fragmented self.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:11.060
Iryŏp calls this self, small-self,

00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:13.500
the kind of gendered self.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:16.250
Woman, that is small-self as well.

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:19.500
On the other hand, based on Buddhist idea,

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:22.980
Iryŏp claims that once one realizes

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:27.640
a provisional nature of this
limited vision of the self,

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:29.930
one finds the Buddhist no-self,

00:36:29.930 --> 00:36:33.683
a self that has capacity to
creatively engage with the life.

00:36:34.995 --> 00:36:37.873
That is what is called great-self.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.590
So here, the great-I is I who realize

00:36:42.590 --> 00:36:45.770
that women's namelessness
in patriarchal society

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:47.853
is not their real self.

00:36:49.220 --> 00:36:52.600
One has enormous capacity
to create one's identity,

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:56.930
which is much bigger than
any kind of confinement

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:59.530
that is imposed by human body

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:04.040
or physical environment
or social norms, right?

00:37:04.040 --> 00:37:06.560
So the great-I is a
vision of the individual

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:09.703
who has a capacity to challenge
the mainstream discourse,

00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:13.700
which imposes a fixed
identity to individuals

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:16.170
based on the categories an individual

00:37:16.170 --> 00:37:19.860
might have to belong to based
on one's society, right?

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:24.140
So for Kim Iryŏp, the
patriarchal Korean society's

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:27.780
imposing of the gender identity "woman"

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:29.330
is a small-self, right?

00:37:29.330 --> 00:37:31.380
But Kim Iryŏp thought that everybody,

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:32.470
it's not only just women,

00:37:32.470 --> 00:37:35.740
everybody has a capacity to
creatively engage with life

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:37.720
in their own way.

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:40.500
If we translate, interpret this,

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:44.930
the claim of Kim Iryŏp in 1960s,

00:37:44.930 --> 00:37:48.480
to our 21st century American society,

00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:50.070
then we can say that great-I

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:53.930
is the I that can challenge
the discriminatory treatment

00:37:53.930 --> 00:37:56.770
of one's identity, be it
related to gender, race,

00:37:56.770 --> 00:37:59.670
sexual orientation, or social class.

00:37:59.670 --> 00:38:04.220
So great-I has to do with the power

00:38:04.220 --> 00:38:08.970
and strength to define
one's own self freely.

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:10.390
And in doing that,

00:38:10.390 --> 00:38:13.410
definitely one needed to
challenge the status quo

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.660
because status quo does not always

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.860
kind of give you the life
and definition of the self

00:38:18.860 --> 00:38:20.913
you think who you are.

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:25.410
So in a way, I think this is
a very strong kind of argument

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:27.130
Kim Iryŏp is making

00:38:27.130 --> 00:38:31.493
after her kind of years
of Buddhist practice.

00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:34.650
And she defines

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:38.720
the characteristics of the
great-self as creativity.

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:40.610
And I think it makes a lot of sense

00:38:40.610 --> 00:38:45.610
because if you want to
engage with your identity

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:46.693
and your life,

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:51.430
away from the kind of a fixed identity

00:38:51.430 --> 00:38:52.830
imposed by the society,

00:38:52.830 --> 00:38:55.493
you need a creative engagement with life.

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:58.840
And the exercise of creativity

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:00.870
is Iryŏp's vision of Buddhist practice

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:02.960
and also her advice to women

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:05.573
who search for freedom and authentic self.

00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:09.650
Here I kind of mix two
very important aspects

00:39:09.650 --> 00:39:10.980
of Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism.

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.210
The first one is that
exercise of creativity

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:15.280
is Kim Iryŏp's vision
of Buddhist practice,

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:18.620
and not many Buddhist
practitioners or Buddhist scholars

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:22.550
or Buddhist teachers use
this expression, creativity,

00:39:22.550 --> 00:39:26.570
as a kind of way of practicing Buddhism.

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:31.210
And one of the interesting
parts Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism

00:39:31.210 --> 00:39:34.630
is that her interpretation
of Buddhism is very unique

00:39:34.630 --> 00:39:38.520
in her use of language,
expressions like this.

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:40.290
I don't know where it came from.

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:43.130
Partly I think it also has to do with

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:45.290
her background in Christianity,

00:39:45.290 --> 00:39:48.360
but I'm yet to find all
the kind of influence,

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:51.053
or simply her genius, right?

00:39:51.940 --> 00:39:54.170
Also, her advice to women's issues.

00:39:54.170 --> 00:39:56.320
So she is advising women,

00:39:56.320 --> 00:39:59.470
if you want to have freedom from gender

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:02.410
in a patriarchal society,
you first need to be able

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:04.870
to find your own self, the creativity.

00:40:04.870 --> 00:40:09.870
And Kim Iryŏp even interpret
the Buddha in this way.

00:40:10.190 --> 00:40:11.107
So I'm quoting:

00:40:11.107 --> 00:40:15.260
"The Buddha is a being who
fully exercised the 'creativity'

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:17.850
with which each being was endowed.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:19.780
A creator is not a special being.

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:22.120
Even an insect can be a creator

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:24.647
if it recovers its original mind."

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:28.730
So this is a kind of full declaration

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:32.170
about the individual's capacity

00:40:32.170 --> 00:40:36.610
to form his, her, their own life

00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:39.863
without being constrained
by external forces.

00:40:42.180 --> 00:40:45.597
And she also uses this
expression "culture."

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:51.490
When creativity, creative
activity really get together

00:40:51.490 --> 00:40:55.143
and appear in a more physical
form, it becomes culture.

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:58.887
So she said this quote:

00:40:58.887 --> 00:41:02.080
"I was aware that to
be a person of culture

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:04.470
meant to be a direct
disciple of the Buddha,

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:06.821
who is the 'great person of culture,'

00:41:06.821 --> 00:41:08.889
and that was why I joined the monastery."

00:41:08.889 --> 00:41:12.845
And this is another very
Kim Iryŏp-like expression.

00:41:12.845 --> 00:41:15.450
The Buddha is "the great
person of culture."

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:17.860
You know, which means, for Kim Iryŏp,

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:20.810
Buddha is the one who 100% exercise

00:41:20.810 --> 00:41:22.973
his creativity, freedom.

00:41:26.270 --> 00:41:31.270
Now, here Kim Iryŏp kind of
goes back to Christianity.

00:41:31.960 --> 00:41:35.930
I told you that Kim Iryŏp
gave up Christianity

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:39.160
late-teenage years, and she
didn't talk about Christianity

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:42.553
further (indistinct) until late 1950s.

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:46.920
At that time, she was
already in her sixties.

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.110
And partly, I think,

00:41:48.110 --> 00:41:51.190
that has to do with her
relationship with her father.

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:54.210
Her father was a faithful Christian.

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:56.210
Christianity is her father's life,

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:59.850
and Kim Iryŏp had an
unusually close relationship

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:01.480
with her father.

00:42:01.480 --> 00:42:03.730
Some of you might know
that in Confucian society,

00:42:03.730 --> 00:42:06.590
father figure is very kind of distant.

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:08.210
Kim Iryŏp and Kim Iryŏp's father,

00:42:08.210 --> 00:42:09.810
they were very close.

00:42:09.810 --> 00:42:13.100
And it looks like, for Kim Iryŏp,

00:42:13.100 --> 00:42:16.890
criticizing Christianity
would've been something similar

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:19.600
to criticizing her father's
entire life, right?

00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:21.730
Perhaps because of that and other reasons,

00:42:21.730 --> 00:42:23.930
she didn't talk about
Christianity for a long time.

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:26.050
And by the end of the 1950s,

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:28.030
perhaps she now felt confident.

00:42:28.030 --> 00:42:30.980
She talked about God and
redefined Christianity and God

00:42:30.980 --> 00:42:32.340
from Buddhist perspective.

00:42:32.340 --> 00:42:36.000
And she says, "God is not
a creator of the world

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:39.780
but a being that fully
exercised creativity."

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:42.060
And she said, "If you say that God

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:43.370
is the creator of the world,

00:42:43.370 --> 00:42:46.650
then you are kind of insulting
God, because in that case,

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:49.940
God is responsible for all
the bad things in the world."

00:42:49.940 --> 00:42:52.610
Obviously, theologians have
all kind of explanation

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:55.600
about this, but that's Kim
Iryŏp's kind of approach.

00:42:55.600 --> 00:42:57.330
And she put God and the Buddha

00:42:57.330 --> 00:43:00.040
in a kind of same kind of a category,

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:01.910
and saying that the Buddha and God

00:43:01.910 --> 00:43:04.073
originate from the same seed.

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:07.730
So God is not the object of one's worship,

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:11.110
but, rather, a model or
evidence that a being is,

00:43:11.110 --> 00:43:14.543
in its ultimate sense,
wholesome and not fragmented.

00:43:16.300 --> 00:43:19.430
At the end of her life, at the last book,

00:43:19.430 --> 00:43:23.310
which was published in 1964,
and Kim Iryŏp died in 1971,

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:26.510
she's kind of focusing

00:43:26.510 --> 00:43:29.710
on this expression "life
force" (speaking in Korean).

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:31.080
At the final stage of her life,

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:34.477
Iryŏp focuses her philosophy
on "life" or "life force."

00:43:35.657 --> 00:43:38.290
"Life" is what is shared
by all the beings,

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:41.200
human, non-human, even inanimate beings,

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:44.140
and that is a foundation of existence.

00:43:44.140 --> 00:43:47.280
To be aware of one's existence
through its foundation,

00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:48.800
the life force, is one's way

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:51.900
to get engaged with one's
existential ecology.

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:53.430
By focusing on the life,

00:43:53.430 --> 00:43:56.830
which is really bottom
foundation of existence,

00:43:56.830 --> 00:44:01.250
Iryŏp was claiming the
equality of all beings.

00:44:01.250 --> 00:44:05.070
And in this case, all beings
include not only humans,

00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:10.070
human animal, nonhuman animals,
and inanimate beings, right?

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:11.400
She's talking about,

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:15.590
even a kind of piece of
broken tile has freedom.

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:17.220
So this is a kind of a,

00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:18.530
it's a symbolic statement,

00:44:18.530 --> 00:44:21.800
but it really tells us her confidence

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:25.137
about the equality of all
existing beings, right?

00:44:27.620 --> 00:44:31.230
So because she was

00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:34.710
such a kind of prominent figure

00:44:34.710 --> 00:44:37.880
in women's movement before
she joined the monastery,

00:44:37.880 --> 00:44:40.240
one of the questions constantly raised

00:44:40.240 --> 00:44:43.970
about Kim Iryŏp's life and
thought, is this question,

00:44:43.970 --> 00:44:46.180
what happened to women's
movement to Kim Iryŏp

00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:48.210
after she joined the monastery?

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:52.230
In fact, she never explicitly
talked about women's movement

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:54.180
after she joined the monastery,

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:56.350
even though I read Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism

00:44:56.350 --> 00:44:58.583
in a very much kind of
feminist perspective.

00:44:59.500 --> 00:45:04.500
Now, there's another way
of approaching this issue.

00:45:04.630 --> 00:45:07.390
I mean, women's movements

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:11.130
can be done in various
different ways, right?

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.610
The fightings are done in
various different ways.

00:45:13.610 --> 00:45:15.960
Some people go on the street and protest.

00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:17.460
Some people can write a book.

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:21.460
Some people can do something
else to promote certain agenda.

00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:24.420
And I think Kim Iryŏp's
path was in a different way.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:27.360
So here is one way of
explaining Kim Iryŏp's position

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:29.193
about women's movement.

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:34.973
When Kim Iryŏp joined
the monastery in 1928,

00:45:35.840 --> 00:45:40.379
no, 1933, her master, Zen Master Man'gong,

00:45:40.379 --> 00:45:44.667
who was very well-known Sŏn
master at the time, told her,

00:45:44.667 --> 00:45:48.240
"I know you are a very
well-known writer and speaker

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:50.330
in the secular world outside.

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:53.960
If you join the monastery, you
don't read, you don't write.

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:54.847
Can you do it?"

00:45:55.810 --> 00:45:56.750
I mean, if you are a writer,

00:45:56.750 --> 00:45:59.020
you understand how difficult it is

00:45:59.020 --> 00:46:01.200
for a writer not writing, right?

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:03.030
Kim Iryŏp said, "Yes."

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:06.970
And then she didn't publish, at least,

00:46:06.970 --> 00:46:10.330
until late 1950s, so about.

00:46:10.330 --> 00:46:11.270
She maintained it.

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:13.670
She kept this promise
for about two decades.

00:46:13.670 --> 00:46:15.470
I was asked when I had an interview

00:46:15.470 --> 00:46:19.217
with Kim Iryŏp's disciple, Wŏlsong sŭnim,

00:46:19.217 --> 00:46:21.590
"Did she really didn't write anything?"

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:25.240
And then Wŏlsong sŭnim told me

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:28.600
that it looks like she wrote something,

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:31.810
but she never showed
her writings to anybody.

00:46:31.810 --> 00:46:33.253
She didn't publish, right?

00:46:34.290 --> 00:46:36.063
So anyway, she kept her promise.

00:46:37.720 --> 00:46:42.720
Now, starting from late 1950s,
she began to publish again.

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:46.855
And the book that I translated,

00:46:46.855 --> 00:46:48.257
(speaking in Korean) in Korean,

00:46:48.257 --> 00:46:52.530
"Reflections of a Zen Buddhist
Nun," was published in 1960,

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:55.459
and then another book,
"Having Burned Away My Youth,"

00:46:55.459 --> 00:46:58.620
(speaking in Korean),
was published in 1962.

00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:00.870
And then the last book,
(speaking in Korean),

00:47:01.907 --> 00:47:04.247
"In Between Happiness and Misfortune,"

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:07.960
was published in 1964.

00:47:07.960 --> 00:47:10.743
Now, if you read these books,

00:47:11.700 --> 00:47:16.190
you kind of, at first you get confused.

00:47:16.190 --> 00:47:18.330
When I first read her
book, I was confused.

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:20.730
I'm reading a Buddhist nun's book.

00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:23.480
And what you read there
is her love affairs.

00:47:23.480 --> 00:47:24.330
Obviously, that happened

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:26.080
before she joined the monastery, right?

00:47:26.080 --> 00:47:28.100
But she talk about her love affairs,

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:31.210
her friends' love affairs,
and so on and so forth.

00:47:31.210 --> 00:47:33.730
What is going on here, right? (chuckles)

00:47:33.730 --> 00:47:35.667
Where is Buddhist philosophy, right?

00:47:36.550 --> 00:47:40.970
Now, if you really begin to
rethink about the situation

00:47:40.970 --> 00:47:45.450
and try to digest what was
Kim Iryŏp trying to do here,

00:47:45.450 --> 00:47:49.483
then you can get some
different kind of inspiration.

00:47:50.710 --> 00:47:53.690
In the book, Kim Iryŏp
kind of talk a lot about

00:47:53.690 --> 00:47:57.060
the life stories of various people,

00:47:57.060 --> 00:47:58.410
including her own, right?

00:47:58.410 --> 00:48:02.040
Lives and deaths of her family
members, her love affairs,

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:04.860
her friends' love affairs,

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:07.010
and the practice of
Christianity and Buddhism.

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:09.413
So in a way, it is biographical writing,

00:48:10.360 --> 00:48:11.337
autobiographical writing,

00:48:11.337 --> 00:48:13.723
biographical writing for her friends.

00:48:14.660 --> 00:48:16.960
But once you begin to read

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:19.410
and think about the meaning of it,

00:48:19.410 --> 00:48:21.740
and then, in between,
she kind of talk about

00:48:21.740 --> 00:48:24.317
Buddhist philosophy, Buddhist doctrines.

00:48:24.317 --> 00:48:27.733
At the beginning of the 1960 publication,

00:48:27.733 --> 00:48:30.470
"Reflections of a Zen
Buddhist Nun," she says that,

00:48:30.470 --> 00:48:32.640
in the preface, "Well, nowadays,

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:35.610
people do not want to think
about the serious things.

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:37.290
If we are trying to talk about religion,

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:39.460
they don't wanna read it.

00:48:39.460 --> 00:48:42.760
So in order to kind of keep my reader,

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:47.580
I mixed my personal stories
with Buddhist doctrine,

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:50.760
and I made this bibimbap,"
right? (chuckles)

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:53.720
So that was her expression,

00:48:55.840 --> 00:48:59.800
but I think there is a lot more than that.

00:48:59.800 --> 00:49:03.110
So if you read the things (indistinct)

00:49:03.110 --> 00:49:06.910
and then you begin to realize
that the diverse topics,

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:08.750
along with Iryŏp's unique way

00:49:08.750 --> 00:49:11.290
of delivering Buddhist philosophy,

00:49:11.290 --> 00:49:15.340
mark her books as an alternative
form of doing philosophy,

00:49:15.340 --> 00:49:19.510
drawn from women's lives in the
milieus of daily experience.

00:49:19.510 --> 00:49:23.020
If you are familiar with
a philosophy textbook,

00:49:23.020 --> 00:49:25.090
the kind of standard philosophy book

00:49:25.090 --> 00:49:29.000
you read in philosophy course,
there's no personal lives.

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:31.930
Philosophy is supposed to be about ideas

00:49:31.930 --> 00:49:34.487
because philosophy should be universal.

00:49:35.594 --> 00:49:38.760
Personal lives, personal
experience, is particular.

00:49:38.760 --> 00:49:39.870
They cannot be universal.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:43.660
That is a main assumption,
especially in modern European,

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:45.163
modern Western philosophy.

00:49:46.230 --> 00:49:49.573
And if you apply that,

00:49:50.570 --> 00:49:51.910
then you see that

00:49:54.360 --> 00:49:57.130
there are many kind of aspects of life

00:49:57.130 --> 00:50:00.800
that should be excluded in
philosophical discourse.

00:50:00.800 --> 00:50:02.810
Life is not always about ideas.

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:06.360
There are a lot of messy things
going on in everyday life.

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:08.620
So Iryŏp writings are her way

00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:10.960
of remembering existence in words.

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:13.100
By retelling her own life story

00:50:13.100 --> 00:50:15.000
and those of her female friends,

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:19.640
Iryŏp made women's lives visible, right?

00:50:19.640 --> 00:50:21.253
That is what writing does.

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:26.330
Your life is written, and then it's there,

00:50:26.330 --> 00:50:27.680
instead of being forgotten,

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:31.920
showing that a woman's
life is not a disposable

00:50:31.920 --> 00:50:35.720
and forgettable component
in a patriarchal society.

00:50:35.720 --> 00:50:38.740
Her narratives are the
witnesses to her life

00:50:38.740 --> 00:50:41.080
and the lives of other women.

00:50:41.080 --> 00:50:45.490
This was Iryŏp's way of
engaging with women's issues.

00:50:45.490 --> 00:50:48.020
And I think this is important aspects

00:50:48.020 --> 00:50:50.110
of promoting women's agenda,

00:50:50.110 --> 00:50:53.507
as much as explicitly saying that

00:50:53.507 --> 00:50:55.770
"I'm engaged with women's movement,"

00:50:55.770 --> 00:50:57.600
or go out and do protest.

00:50:57.600 --> 00:51:00.150
There are different ways
of doing a protest, right?

00:51:01.300 --> 00:51:06.040
So here I kind of place Kim
Iryŏp's life and thought

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:09.440
at three different kind of dimensions.

00:51:09.440 --> 00:51:11.710
First, Kim Iryŏp's life and thought,

00:51:11.710 --> 00:51:13.950
in the context of women and Buddhism,

00:51:13.950 --> 00:51:15.880
which was the title of my talk.

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:18.820
And then, after that, you can think about,

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:21.860
in the context of intellectual
history of modern Korea,

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:24.740
because, definitely, Kim
Iryŏp should be there

00:51:24.740 --> 00:51:26.970
in the intellectual history of Korea,

00:51:26.970 --> 00:51:29.500
which has not been the case.

00:51:29.500 --> 00:51:33.360
And then further, in the
context of the position

00:51:33.360 --> 00:51:36.587
of non-Western philosophy
and women's philosophy

00:51:36.587 --> 00:51:41.587
vis-à-vis male-centered and
West-centered philosophy.

00:51:42.040 --> 00:51:43.790
I mean, if you have taken
any philosophy course,

00:51:43.790 --> 00:51:47.570
tell me, how many women's
writings have you read?

00:51:47.570 --> 00:51:51.450
How many non-Western
writings have you read?

00:51:51.450 --> 00:51:54.850
How many non-Western women's
writings have you read?

00:51:54.850 --> 00:51:57.103
Here the answer is clear, right?

00:51:58.370 --> 00:52:02.500
So there are a lot of kind
of philosophical arguments

00:52:02.500 --> 00:52:03.700
you can make with that,

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:05.320
but one thing that you
can really think about

00:52:05.320 --> 00:52:09.820
is what Miranda Fricker says,
that epistemic injustice.

00:52:09.820 --> 00:52:11.500
When we talk about injustice,

00:52:11.500 --> 00:52:13.600
we talk about social injustice,

00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:18.600
when you are mistreated in a
society, in an institution.

00:52:19.210 --> 00:52:23.450
But here, epistemic injustice
is the injustice that happens

00:52:23.450 --> 00:52:28.340
before that social injustice,
visible form, happens.

00:52:28.340 --> 00:52:31.290
In other words, because your mindset,

00:52:31.290 --> 00:52:32.730
before you hear something,

00:52:32.730 --> 00:52:35.780
you already made a
judgment about that person.

00:52:35.780 --> 00:52:38.430
So when we hear about
the sexual harassment,

00:52:38.430 --> 00:52:39.960
we always emphasize this, right,

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:42.670
it's not the victim's fault.

00:52:42.670 --> 00:52:46.410
But there's always kind
of a pre-assumption,

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:47.910
that who's right, who's wrong.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:51.920
So in this book, Miranda
Fricker talk about

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:54.780
two different types of
epistemic injustice.

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:57.060
The first one is hermeneutical injustice.

00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:59.527
The second one is testimonial injustice.

00:52:59.527 --> 00:53:03.540
Hermeneutical injustice has
to do with interpretation.

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:08.360
So quoting, "A gap in collective
interpretive resources

00:53:08.360 --> 00:53:11.180
puts someone at an unfair disadvantage

00:53:11.180 --> 00:53:14.820
when it comes to making sense
of their social experiences."

00:53:14.820 --> 00:53:18.070
So when New Women talk about
their kind of experience

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:20.100
of society and injustice,

00:53:20.100 --> 00:53:22.460
the society already made a judgment.

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:24.230
That's epistemic injustice, right?

00:53:24.230 --> 00:53:27.003
Hermeneutical injustice. They
are not gonna listen to them.

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:30.870
The second one is testimonial injustice,

00:53:30.870 --> 00:53:35.290
which occurs, quoting, "when
prejudice causes a hearer

00:53:35.290 --> 00:53:38.300
to give a deflated level of credibility

00:53:38.300 --> 00:53:40.020
to a speaker's word."

00:53:40.020 --> 00:53:42.410
In other words, you hear
some woman's experience

00:53:42.410 --> 00:53:46.190
and, "Okay, that makes sense,
but that's not as important

00:53:46.190 --> 00:53:51.040
as what is said by my male
colleague," for example.

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:54.720
This is what we always,
still, kind of experience.

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:58.160
And then, the New Women
phenomenon in Korea

00:53:58.160 --> 00:54:01.020
or Kim Iryŏp's life itself

00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:05.300
really shows us how this
type of epistemic injustice

00:54:05.300 --> 00:54:09.570
really kind of constrained women's life

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:12.643
and many people who are at the margin.

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:16.464
In a patriarchal Korean Buddhism,

00:54:16.464 --> 00:54:18.690
Iryŏp's position in modern Korean Buddhism

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:21.420
demonstrates the patriarchal
nature of Korean Buddhism

00:54:21.420 --> 00:54:23.300
that continues to refuse to acknowledge

00:54:23.300 --> 00:54:25.920
the importance of women's
search for identity

00:54:25.920 --> 00:54:28.863
and existential value, and
the practice continues today.

00:54:31.950 --> 00:54:34.600
In Korea, I wrote in English.

00:54:34.600 --> 00:54:38.340
So I published a book
about Kim Iryŏp in English.

00:54:38.340 --> 00:54:40.320
But in Korea, there is no publication

00:54:40.320 --> 00:54:41.820
about Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism.

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:44.260
Nobody studies it, right?

00:54:44.260 --> 00:54:48.400
Even nowadays, they do not
think Kim Iryŏp is important.

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:52.830
She's just somebody who
talk about love affairs.

00:54:52.830 --> 00:54:56.657
So when I translate that
book, Kim Iryŏp's first book,

00:54:56.657 --> 00:54:58.317
"Reflections of a Zen Master,"

00:55:00.360 --> 00:55:01.400
that book was supported

00:55:01.400 --> 00:55:05.110
by Korean kind of government project.

00:55:05.110 --> 00:55:08.587
When that book was selected,
in Korea, people said,

00:55:08.587 --> 00:55:10.540
"Why do we need to translate a woman

00:55:10.540 --> 00:55:12.007
who talk about love affairs?"

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:15.230
The only reason that book survived

00:55:15.230 --> 00:55:18.490
was the person who's in
charge of that translation

00:55:18.490 --> 00:55:22.200
was Robert Buswell, who
Prof. Kim mentioned,

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:24.340
the big figure in Korean Buddhism.

00:55:24.340 --> 00:55:26.550
And Robert Buswell
said, "We should do it."

00:55:26.550 --> 00:55:28.350
And Korean people just said, "Yes," right?

00:55:28.350 --> 00:55:30.800
Otherwise, I don't think
it will ever happen,

00:55:30.800 --> 00:55:33.870
which shows that still

00:55:33.870 --> 00:55:36.410
there's no kind of a scholarship

00:55:36.410 --> 00:55:38.263
on Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism itself.

00:55:40.210 --> 00:55:41.990
And silencing and silenced.

00:55:41.990 --> 00:55:44.810
The voices of the New Women, or of women,

00:55:44.810 --> 00:55:46.970
have been silenced in Korean society,

00:55:46.970 --> 00:55:51.970
not only in the 1920s but
for decades until the 1990s,

00:55:51.990 --> 00:55:55.400
when women scholars
began to rediscover them.

00:55:55.400 --> 00:56:00.400
So New Women's stories were not discussed

00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:04.200
in academic setting until 1990s in Korea.

00:56:04.200 --> 00:56:08.810
In between, in Korea,
there was always excuses.

00:56:08.810 --> 00:56:11.100
There was colonialism,
there was Korean War,

00:56:11.100 --> 00:56:14.590
there was economic development,
and so on and so forth,

00:56:14.590 --> 00:56:18.210
which should be more
important than women's issue,

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:21.210
which is nothing from Korean perspective.

00:56:21.210 --> 00:56:24.296
That's why that women's voice is not there

00:56:24.296 --> 00:56:26.963
in intellectual history
of modern Korea, right?

00:56:28.070 --> 00:56:30.160
Where do we find the women's voices

00:56:30.160 --> 00:56:32.520
in intellectual history of modern Korea?

00:56:32.520 --> 00:56:36.050
So Iryŏp writings and life
reveal one of the missing parts

00:56:36.050 --> 00:56:37.543
in this history.

00:56:39.950 --> 00:56:42.783
And the third aspect

00:56:42.783 --> 00:56:46.040
is the new style of philosophizing.

00:56:46.040 --> 00:56:50.640
So as Prof. Kim mentioned
before, when he introduced me,

00:56:50.640 --> 00:56:54.733
I call Kim Iryŏp's philosophy,
narrative philosophy.

00:56:55.930 --> 00:56:57.810
So narrative philosophy is not something

00:56:57.810 --> 00:57:00.060
just to kind of discuss ideas,

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:03.890
but this is a philosophy
in which we talk about,

00:57:03.890 --> 00:57:06.080
narrate about one's experience,

00:57:06.080 --> 00:57:08.100
and from there, trying to construct

00:57:08.100 --> 00:57:10.177
the meaning of existence.

00:57:10.177 --> 00:57:13.440
One of the reasons women
have been undervalued

00:57:13.440 --> 00:57:15.410
in the traditional philosophy

00:57:15.410 --> 00:57:19.250
is because women's life
events have been taken

00:57:19.250 --> 00:57:21.120
out of the context of their lives

00:57:21.120 --> 00:57:24.330
and become objects of judgment
based on the standards

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:26.790
established from patriarchal perspective.

00:57:26.790 --> 00:57:28.250
Therefore, the women's stories

00:57:28.250 --> 00:57:30.780
are always about household life,

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:32.940
and then that's nothing.

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:35.450
This kind of attitude really pushed women

00:57:35.450 --> 00:57:38.220
outside of domain of philosophy.

00:57:38.220 --> 00:57:40.100
The non-Western philosophy and religion

00:57:40.100 --> 00:57:43.070
in the Western academia has
been treated as inferior

00:57:43.070 --> 00:57:45.860
because they do not fit it
in the standard and the style

00:57:45.860 --> 00:57:48.140
of Western philosophy.

00:57:48.140 --> 00:57:50.030
But why should Western
philosophy be the standard

00:57:50.030 --> 00:57:52.470
for philosophizing, right?

00:57:52.470 --> 00:57:56.220
And Iryŏp's books show us how
narrative of women's lives

00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:58.340
help us understand women's lives

00:57:58.340 --> 00:58:00.130
and different forms of meaning production,

00:58:00.130 --> 00:58:02.073
based on the life stories.

00:58:03.470 --> 00:58:07.230
So in a way, just to
kind of, as a conclusion,

00:58:07.230 --> 00:58:09.370
our research on Kim Iryŏp awakens us

00:58:09.370 --> 00:58:11.400
to the double or triple discrimination

00:58:11.400 --> 00:58:13.563
in our society and scholarship.

00:58:14.670 --> 00:58:16.370
In the context of Korean Studies,

00:58:16.370 --> 00:58:18.890
the complete disregard
of Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism

00:58:18.890 --> 00:58:21.940
in Korean Buddhist community
and Buddhist scholarship

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:23.840
demonstrates the patriarchal nature

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:25.780
of Buddhism practiced in Korea,

00:58:25.780 --> 00:58:27.900
as well as in the Korean
Buddhist scholarship

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:29.083
and academic community.

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:32.830
In the context of intellectual
history of modern Korea,

00:58:32.830 --> 00:58:36.020
silencing of women's voices
reveal the incapacity

00:58:36.020 --> 00:58:38.520
of Korean society and scholarship

00:58:38.520 --> 00:58:41.550
to deal with diversity of human endeavor

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:44.900
to creatively find meaning
in existence and community.

00:58:44.900 --> 00:58:46.550
So I think the kind of silence

00:58:46.550 --> 00:58:49.360
and the tradition of
silencing different voices

00:58:49.360 --> 00:58:52.640
throughout 20th century, obviously kind of

00:58:54.240 --> 00:58:57.120
lack Korean society the capacity

00:58:57.120 --> 00:59:00.140
to deal with these different
voices now in 21st century,

00:59:00.140 --> 00:59:02.090
and society became more and more rigid.

00:59:03.460 --> 00:59:06.240
On the other hand, Iryŏp's
Buddhism offers us a way

00:59:06.240 --> 00:59:07.930
for women to engage with Buddhism

00:59:07.930 --> 00:59:10.300
in their search for freedom and identity.

00:59:10.300 --> 00:59:13.040
So Buddhism can be thought of

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:15.230
within the context of a
woman's search for freedom.

00:59:15.230 --> 00:59:18.180
Iryŏp's narrative philosophy and Buddhism

00:59:18.180 --> 00:59:21.520
that is based on the experience
of women's daily life

00:59:21.520 --> 00:59:24.860
offers an alternative to
the dominant philosophy

00:59:24.860 --> 00:59:27.160
and thought tradition in the academia

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:29.910
that exclusively validates West-centered

00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:32.134
and male-dominated philosophizing.

00:59:32.134 --> 00:59:33.694
Thank you.

00:59:33.694 --> 00:59:36.861
(audience applauding)

00:59:38.914 --> 00:59:43.239
- [Moderator] Thank you so
much for such an engaging talk.

00:59:43.239 --> 00:59:45.989
Yeah, let's go over to questions.

00:59:50.787 --> 00:59:52.140
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:54.050
This was really interesting.

00:59:54.050 --> 00:59:56.920
I would really love to
hear more about her concept

00:59:56.920 --> 00:59:58.880
of the great-I.

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:00.990
Just how does she sort
of explain this concept?

01:00:00.990 --> 01:00:03.890
Is it something similar
to, like, Buddha-nature?

01:00:03.890 --> 01:00:06.820
Is she engaging like a kind of absolute

01:00:06.820 --> 01:00:08.980
versus conventional truth binary?

01:00:08.980 --> 01:00:12.780
Like, yeah, what is the
great-I that she explains?

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:14.680
- That's a very good question, right?

01:00:14.680 --> 01:00:17.690
So you used this kind of a
Buddhist vocabulary, right?

01:00:17.690 --> 01:00:20.760
Conventional and ultimate,
or the Buddha-nature.

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:25.420
I think that is what
Kim Iryŏp was thinking.

01:00:25.420 --> 01:00:27.340
But interesting, another interesting part

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:28.173
of Kim Iryŏp's Buddhism

01:00:28.173 --> 01:00:30.890
is that she rarely uses
Buddhist vocabulary.

01:00:30.890 --> 01:00:33.170
She uses it in her own way.

01:00:33.170 --> 01:00:37.870
So great-I, I explained the great-I here

01:00:37.870 --> 01:00:41.650
a little bit more
socially-engaged concept.

01:00:41.650 --> 01:00:44.930
She put it in a very
abstract way sometimes,

01:00:44.930 --> 01:00:49.930
which is great-I is the I of nothingness.

01:00:50.420 --> 01:00:51.570
So that is part of it, right?

01:00:51.570 --> 01:00:54.860
So in a way, it has to do with emptiness,

01:00:54.860 --> 01:00:58.120
total openness of being.

01:00:58.120 --> 01:01:02.220
So one of the expressions she used

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:05.410
is the root of nothingness,
(speaking in Korean),

01:01:05.410 --> 01:01:08.790
the root of nothingness, that
is a foundation of existence,

01:01:08.790 --> 01:01:10.650
and that is great-I,

01:01:10.650 --> 01:01:13.810
which is I

01:01:13.810 --> 01:01:16.993
before the thought of I arises.

01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:19.673
Does it make sense?

01:01:19.673 --> 01:01:22.390
I, openness-I, before the thought of I

01:01:22.390 --> 01:01:24.233
are letting in the second I,

01:01:24.233 --> 01:01:27.113
then you have a kind of boundary arises.

01:01:27.970 --> 01:01:29.670
And she uses this expression a lot:

01:01:29.670 --> 01:01:32.550
a thought before a thought arises.

01:01:32.550 --> 01:01:36.440
So the first thought is
this kind of total openness

01:01:36.440 --> 01:01:39.110
of our (indistinct) thought thinking,

01:01:39.110 --> 01:01:42.500
whereas thought before a thought arises...

01:01:42.500 --> 01:01:46.210
When thought arises, already
this thought was compromised.

01:01:46.210 --> 01:01:48.760
We already see things from
kind of our own perspective.

01:01:48.760 --> 01:01:49.840
When I look at the water bottle,

01:01:49.840 --> 01:01:52.290
I already see it as "I
can drink water," right?

01:01:52.290 --> 01:01:55.283
Instead of, "I don't know
what water bottle want to be

01:01:55.283 --> 01:01:56.840
(chuckles) except it being drinken by me."

01:01:56.840 --> 01:01:58.300
But that kind of idea, right?

01:01:58.300 --> 01:01:59.810
Total openness.

01:01:59.810 --> 01:02:02.100
So, yeah, so that's true.

01:02:02.100 --> 01:02:05.940
It could be Buddha-nature, and
in an ultimate sense, right,

01:02:05.940 --> 01:02:07.200
at the ultimate level.

01:02:07.200 --> 01:02:10.030
So conventional level perhaps is limited,

01:02:10.030 --> 01:02:11.390
but then ultimate level...

01:02:11.390 --> 01:02:14.360
But she rarely uses that
Buddhist expressions,

01:02:14.360 --> 01:02:15.533
Buddhist vocabularies.

01:02:16.515 --> 01:02:17.348
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

01:02:17.348 --> 01:02:18.181
- Sure.

01:02:20.235 --> 01:02:22.818
- [Moderator] Another question?

01:02:24.862 --> 01:02:26.840
- [Audience Member] Thank you
so much for speaking with us.

01:02:26.840 --> 01:02:30.220
I just wanted to ask,
could you talk more about

01:02:30.220 --> 01:02:32.023
what were the influences in the 1950s

01:02:32.023 --> 01:02:34.790
that made Kim Iryŏp start writing again

01:02:34.790 --> 01:02:36.353
and trying to share her thoughts?

01:02:36.353 --> 01:02:39.290
- Mm-mm, mm-mm. That's a good question.

01:02:39.290 --> 01:02:44.290
So my interpretation is that, by 1950s,

01:02:44.700 --> 01:02:48.650
I think Iryŏp became
confident about herself.

01:02:48.650 --> 01:02:50.780
So when she joined the monastery,

01:02:50.780 --> 01:02:53.740
so she joined the monastery in 1933,

01:02:53.740 --> 01:02:58.740
that year, she had an interview
with the journal called

01:02:59.427 --> 01:03:01.293
"Opening of the World," the "Kyebyŏk."

01:03:01.293 --> 01:03:05.147
And there, she said something like,

01:03:05.147 --> 01:03:07.280
"I became, I practice Buddhism

01:03:07.280 --> 01:03:11.650
so then I can be a better
writer, like the Buddha."

01:03:11.650 --> 01:03:15.320
So she thought that
writing, doing the writing

01:03:15.320 --> 01:03:18.070
before you really, fully
become a human being,

01:03:18.070 --> 01:03:19.857
that's nothing, you should not do it.

01:03:19.857 --> 01:03:21.380
"So I'm practicing Buddhism,

01:03:21.380 --> 01:03:24.830
so then I can be a great writer
like the Buddha himself."

01:03:24.830 --> 01:03:27.910
So she invested this time, about 20 years,

01:03:27.910 --> 01:03:29.420
more than 20 years,

01:03:29.420 --> 01:03:31.670
to really think about
what it means to exist,

01:03:32.810 --> 01:03:35.180
what is freedom, and so on and so forth.

01:03:35.180 --> 01:03:40.087
So by mid-1950s, she
must have felt confident,

01:03:40.087 --> 01:03:44.360
"Now I can do the writing without kind of

01:03:44.360 --> 01:03:49.000
being tempted by fame,"

01:03:49.000 --> 01:03:50.090
and so on and so forth.

01:03:50.090 --> 01:03:51.230
I mean, if you are a writer,

01:03:51.230 --> 01:03:53.507
partly there is also that
kind of aspect, right?

01:03:53.507 --> 01:03:56.450
"I want to be famous, I
want to be a celebrity,"

01:03:56.450 --> 01:03:57.840
and so on and so forth.

01:03:57.840 --> 01:04:01.237
So I think that by the
time she's in her sixties

01:04:01.237 --> 01:04:03.910
and she became confident about herself,

01:04:03.910 --> 01:04:06.480
and she also talk about
Christianity, right?

01:04:06.480 --> 01:04:09.610
Yeah, so that's my interpretation.

01:04:09.610 --> 01:04:13.077
There is no, as far as I
know, I read almost everything

01:04:13.077 --> 01:04:16.550
Kim Iryŏp wrote that are available, right,

01:04:16.550 --> 01:04:21.490
and there is no indication
that there was any instant

01:04:21.490 --> 01:04:24.110
that made her kind of publish her works.

01:04:24.110 --> 01:04:26.570
So if there's nothing like that,

01:04:26.570 --> 01:04:28.400
then it's a kind of gradual training.

01:04:28.400 --> 01:04:29.890
She trained herself for 20 years,

01:04:29.890 --> 01:04:33.077
and then, by that time,
she perhaps thought that,

01:04:33.077 --> 01:04:35.573
"Well, now I can do it again. Go back."

01:04:35.573 --> 01:04:36.573
Right. Yeah.

01:04:39.080 --> 01:04:40.363
- [Audience Member] I was
also really interested

01:04:40.363 --> 01:04:43.481
in the small-I and great-I
as Buddhist concepts,

01:04:43.481 --> 01:04:46.181
and building on the last
question about that,

01:04:46.181 --> 01:04:48.989
it seems to me like the soa, the small-I,

01:04:48.989 --> 01:04:51.060
is this self that's still constrained

01:04:51.060 --> 01:04:53.470
by the assumption of conventional truth,

01:04:53.470 --> 01:04:56.572
and the tae'a, the great-I, is more,

01:04:56.572 --> 01:04:58.360
(indistinct) still a conventional self,

01:04:58.360 --> 01:05:00.327
because there ultimately is no-self,

01:05:00.327 --> 01:05:01.357
but it's sort of overcome that.

01:05:01.357 --> 01:05:03.170
You've you reached that enlightenment,

01:05:03.170 --> 01:05:04.630
so beyond the (indistinct).
- Right, right, right, right.

01:05:04.630 --> 01:05:06.620
- [Audience Member] So I
think that is very helpful

01:05:06.620 --> 01:05:09.330
in thinking about these
Buddhist terms about, like,

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:11.890
what it is to get beyond
a conventional truth.

01:05:11.890 --> 01:05:14.480
And this was kind of coming
up here in your talk,

01:05:14.480 --> 01:05:15.510
in instances of, like,

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:19.130
overcoming the constraints
of concepts like gender.

01:05:19.130 --> 01:05:20.190
And on the self,

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:21.980
I can see that happening
on the individual level,

01:05:21.980 --> 01:05:24.110
realizing, "Okay, I'm not
constrained by these things,"

01:05:24.110 --> 01:05:26.590
but what about on the larger social level?

01:05:26.590 --> 01:05:29.580
And it's the same problem we
run into with critical theory.

01:05:29.580 --> 01:05:31.040
We can talk about critical theory,

01:05:31.040 --> 01:05:33.840
but if we don't get those
who are out there in society,

01:05:33.840 --> 01:05:35.510
in positions of power,

01:05:35.510 --> 01:05:37.970
positions of influence,
spreading information,

01:05:37.970 --> 01:05:42.970
to see beyond the conventional
truth, that of concepts,

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:44.860
how is the violence stopped getting done?

01:05:44.860 --> 01:05:47.720
Is there anything implicit
or explicit in her writing

01:05:47.720 --> 01:05:48.553
that points to that,

01:05:48.553 --> 01:05:52.200
or is it strictly a
self-enlightenment, cultivation thing?

01:05:52.200 --> 01:05:55.940
- So I can answer that
in two different ways.

01:05:55.940 --> 01:05:58.960
First one, no, she doesn't talk
about social issue that much

01:05:58.960 --> 01:06:00.700
after she joined the monastery, right?

01:06:00.700 --> 01:06:04.860
So her great-I is a very
much individual level

01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:06.640
of enlightenment issue, right?

01:06:06.640 --> 01:06:09.290
But then she also offer it to any woman

01:06:09.290 --> 01:06:10.450
who wants to be free.

01:06:10.450 --> 01:06:15.450
Any individual who want
to lead a creative life

01:06:15.600 --> 01:06:19.250
should really be trying to
exercise this great-I, right?

01:06:19.250 --> 01:06:21.220
So on the one hand,

01:06:21.220 --> 01:06:25.990
that is an invitation to self-cultivation

01:06:25.990 --> 01:06:28.910
so that that person can be a

01:06:33.064 --> 01:06:34.930
valuable member of a society.

01:06:34.930 --> 01:06:37.710
And actually, in one of the book panels

01:06:37.710 --> 01:06:39.570
about Kim Iryŏp's book,

01:06:39.570 --> 01:06:43.097
Leah Kalmanson, who does
Japanese Zen Buddhism, actually,

01:06:43.097 --> 01:06:47.320
she wrote a kind of article
about Kim Iryŏp and feminism.

01:06:47.320 --> 01:06:48.940
And there she said that,

01:06:48.940 --> 01:06:51.820
well, meditation is not an
individual event, right?

01:06:51.820 --> 01:06:55.640
Because by doing meditation,
you want to be a better person.

01:06:55.640 --> 01:06:59.260
You want to achieve a
certain type of state

01:06:59.260 --> 01:07:01.790
which cannot be just limited by yourself,

01:07:01.790 --> 01:07:04.240
especially in Buddhism, when
everything is connected.

01:07:04.240 --> 01:07:08.010
If one person reached a certain state,

01:07:08.010 --> 01:07:10.550
that person's capacity will illuminate

01:07:10.550 --> 01:07:12.860
and have an influence on others.

01:07:12.860 --> 01:07:15.990
Therefore meditation
is a political action.

01:07:15.990 --> 01:07:17.060
That's what she said.

01:07:17.060 --> 01:07:20.590
And in a way, that is a good way of seeing

01:07:20.590 --> 01:07:24.860
the influence of religion
or the self-cultivation,

01:07:24.860 --> 01:07:29.110
even though in our society that
type of invisible influence

01:07:29.110 --> 01:07:30.700
is not that much valued.

01:07:30.700 --> 01:07:32.700
We always want to see something, right,

01:07:32.700 --> 01:07:33.790
and grasp it.

01:07:33.790 --> 01:07:35.540
So a tangible, visible way.

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:37.490
But I think that's an important aspect.

01:07:38.504 --> 01:07:39.840
On the other hand, it's true.

01:07:39.840 --> 01:07:41.810
Kim Iryŏp has been criticized

01:07:41.810 --> 01:07:45.130
for lack of that clear social vision,

01:07:45.130 --> 01:07:46.697
like Zen Buddhism has been, right?

01:07:46.697 --> 01:07:49.930
Zen Buddhism has been
criticized because of that.

01:07:49.930 --> 01:07:52.430
And Kim Iryŏp was
criticized for that, too.

01:07:52.430 --> 01:07:54.010
In a way I'm defending Kim Iryŏp

01:07:54.010 --> 01:07:57.256
to bring this meditation

01:07:57.256 --> 01:08:00.120
as a social, political action,

01:08:00.120 --> 01:08:00.990
and so and so forth.

01:08:00.990 --> 01:08:03.730
I think both are true,
from my perspective.

01:08:03.730 --> 01:08:08.140
So my parent generation, my aunt,

01:08:08.140 --> 01:08:10.970
they said that after Kim Iryŏp's
first book was published,

01:08:10.970 --> 01:08:14.096
many young Korean women joined
the monastery. (chuckles)

01:08:14.096 --> 01:08:15.670
So, you know, that shows

01:08:15.670 --> 01:08:19.040
the kind of influence of this woman.

01:08:19.040 --> 01:08:21.190
And well, actually,

01:08:21.190 --> 01:08:23.890
after I published the
translation of Kim Iryŏp's book,

01:08:25.270 --> 01:08:27.770
the Korean community in
Washington, D.C., where I live,

01:08:27.770 --> 01:08:30.070
they gave me a small book party,

01:08:30.070 --> 01:08:32.050
that everybody-knows-everybody party.

01:08:32.050 --> 01:08:35.161
So about 20 people joined
to celebrate the publication

01:08:35.161 --> 01:08:35.994
of Kim Iryŏp's book.

01:08:35.994 --> 01:08:40.980
But that event was kind of
announced on Korean newspaper.

01:08:40.980 --> 01:08:43.330
It's not a kind of invitation,
but anybody can come.

01:08:43.330 --> 01:08:46.060
But anyway, and then at the party,

01:08:46.060 --> 01:08:49.150
one older lady and young woman came.

01:08:49.150 --> 01:08:51.030
Nobody knew who they were,

01:08:51.030 --> 01:08:52.455
so we were asking,
"Who's that? Who's that?

01:08:52.455 --> 01:08:53.288
Who's that?", right? (chuckles)

01:08:53.288 --> 01:08:58.027
So after kind of a while
later, we asked the lady,

01:08:58.027 --> 01:09:00.880
"So how did you get to
know of this event?"

01:09:00.880 --> 01:09:03.187
And she said, daughter says that,

01:09:03.187 --> 01:09:06.410
"My mother saw the
announcement on the newspaper

01:09:06.410 --> 01:09:09.160
and she told me, 'We should go there.'"

01:09:09.160 --> 01:09:12.490
And then she said, "My
mother doesn't read English."

01:09:12.490 --> 01:09:15.427
But she said, the mother said that,

01:09:15.427 --> 01:09:18.080
"Kim Iryŏp was the woman in my generation

01:09:18.080 --> 01:09:21.200
who led the life which we couldn't do,

01:09:21.200 --> 01:09:22.940
but we wanted to live."

01:09:22.940 --> 01:09:25.740
Kim Iryŏp was the one who
led a life of freedom,

01:09:25.740 --> 01:09:27.910
searching for her own identity.

01:09:27.910 --> 01:09:29.940
Even though she was not
able to live like that,

01:09:29.940 --> 01:09:31.690
but she really admire her.

01:09:31.690 --> 01:09:34.410
Therefore she wanted to
come to book party, right?

01:09:34.410 --> 01:09:37.780
So for me, that was really, really,

01:09:37.780 --> 01:09:39.530
there was a real inspiration.

01:09:39.530 --> 01:09:42.420
I mean, that shows the kind
of influence of Kim Iryŏp

01:09:42.420 --> 01:09:45.640
on a certain generation of Korean women.

01:09:45.640 --> 01:09:47.770
But it was not only Korean women.

01:09:47.770 --> 01:09:48.820
My parent generation,

01:09:48.820 --> 01:09:52.051
male admire Kim Iryŏp as a
kind of cool girl. (chuckles)

01:09:52.051 --> 01:09:53.933
(audience chuckles)
Yeah.

01:09:56.070 --> 01:09:58.140
- [Audience Member] Thank you so much.

01:09:58.140 --> 01:10:01.803
There's a lot to think
about from your comments.

01:10:03.160 --> 01:10:07.203
I'd like to ask a question from
the perspective of language,

01:10:09.500 --> 01:10:14.500
being a writer, language issues,
linguistics more broadly,

01:10:15.480 --> 01:10:18.130
leading into concepts and notions

01:10:18.130 --> 01:10:20.123
from a philosophical perspective.

01:10:21.240 --> 01:10:23.790
Understanding that you
know her work so well,

01:10:23.790 --> 01:10:26.030
I think you're in a position

01:10:26.030 --> 01:10:29.870
to be able to assess or intuit

01:10:29.870 --> 01:10:32.700
to what degree her language itself

01:10:32.700 --> 01:10:37.270
changed over that 20-year hiatus.

01:10:37.270 --> 01:10:40.620
She were wrote before, and
then she wrote afterward,

01:10:40.620 --> 01:10:42.040
and then she wrote afterward

01:10:42.040 --> 01:10:45.253
from a state of having realized something.

01:10:47.090 --> 01:10:51.010
And we all know that language
itself poses the same problems

01:10:51.010 --> 01:10:56.010
that we encounter in a
broader philosophical sense.

01:10:56.150 --> 01:10:57.670
What pronouns you choose.

01:10:57.670 --> 01:10:59.131
We grapple with this today

01:10:59.131 --> 01:11:00.460
in English.
- That's right, yeah.

01:11:00.460 --> 01:11:02.670
- [Audience Member] And every
language family is different.

01:11:02.670 --> 01:11:03.570
- Right, yeah.

01:11:03.570 --> 01:11:05.060
- [Audience Member] But
there are identifiers

01:11:05.060 --> 01:11:06.620
for language spoken by women

01:11:06.620 --> 01:11:09.563
and language spoken by
men across societies.

01:11:10.780 --> 01:11:15.190
Did she, I'm just curious
whether you sensed

01:11:15.190 --> 01:11:16.853
any difference in her writing,

01:11:18.140 --> 01:11:21.723
in the way that she spoke
of herself, to begin with,

01:11:23.070 --> 01:11:27.220
and otherwise, grammatical issues,

01:11:27.220 --> 01:11:31.700
just choices of what pronoun

01:11:31.700 --> 01:11:33.970
to use, perhaps?

01:11:33.970 --> 01:11:34.803
- That's a-

01:11:34.803 --> 01:11:36.290
- [Audience Member] I'm
more familiar with Japanese,

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:37.590
but you can make choices

01:11:37.590 --> 01:11:42.590
about how you refer to
yourself in a more neutral way

01:11:42.730 --> 01:11:44.920
or as a really very feminine way

01:11:44.920 --> 01:11:47.020
of pronouncing something and so on.

01:11:47.020 --> 01:11:49.860
- Right. Yeah, that's a great question.

01:11:49.860 --> 01:11:52.370
Now I think about that issue.

01:11:52.370 --> 01:11:55.810
So before she joined a monastery,

01:11:55.810 --> 01:12:00.370
she wrote mostly poetry or short stories

01:12:00.370 --> 01:12:03.850
and couple of short polemics
about women's issue.

01:12:03.850 --> 01:12:07.500
And there it's more like a
argumentative tone, right,

01:12:07.500 --> 01:12:09.580
and claiming women's issue.

01:12:09.580 --> 01:12:13.610
Or, she faced a lot of
death in the family.

01:12:13.610 --> 01:12:17.110
She lost all the immediate
family before she reached 20.

01:12:17.110 --> 01:12:19.310
So she talked a lot about this agony,

01:12:19.310 --> 01:12:22.940
about losing family members.

01:12:22.940 --> 01:12:26.960
She wrote in poetry. She
wrote in short story.

01:12:26.960 --> 01:12:30.410
So that was before she
joined the monastery.

01:12:30.410 --> 01:12:35.100
So in the later books, I have
to say, there is some things,

01:12:35.100 --> 01:12:37.870
a little bit of urgency in the later book

01:12:37.870 --> 01:12:40.730
when she talk about Buddhism.

01:12:40.730 --> 01:12:44.860
So actually, this semester,

01:12:44.860 --> 01:12:47.470
in my seminar, we read Kim Iryŏp's book,

01:12:47.470 --> 01:12:51.850
and then some student says
that, "She's pushing us.

01:12:51.850 --> 01:12:54.610
She's pushing us and philosophy
shouldn't be that way."

01:12:54.610 --> 01:12:56.400
And I was thinking, "What does that mean,

01:12:56.400 --> 01:12:58.110
that she's pushing us?"

01:12:58.110 --> 01:13:02.150
And in a way, that's what I
think that urgency in that book.

01:13:02.150 --> 01:13:05.480
I mean, "You should do this,
but why are you not doing it?"

01:13:05.480 --> 01:13:10.210
And that is kind of her sense of urgency

01:13:10.210 --> 01:13:14.880
and that's distinctive
in the later writing.

01:13:14.880 --> 01:13:19.520
And she uses a lot of her
own way of expressing things.

01:13:19.520 --> 01:13:22.130
And sometimes, even in Korean,

01:13:22.130 --> 01:13:24.570
sometimes it doesn't make sense, right?

01:13:24.570 --> 01:13:25.403
I mean, I don't know whether

01:13:25.403 --> 01:13:29.840
it was a kind of printing
quality at the time,

01:13:29.840 --> 01:13:31.570
but there's some passages

01:13:31.570 --> 01:13:34.730
in the book that I translated that,

01:13:34.730 --> 01:13:36.050
they didn't make sense in Korean.

01:13:36.050 --> 01:13:39.910
And when you translate it, you
have to translate it, right?

01:13:39.910 --> 01:13:41.980
And I couldn't make sense of it (chuckles)

01:13:41.980 --> 01:13:44.240
from the Korean original.

01:13:44.240 --> 01:13:46.750
So I translated literally,

01:13:46.750 --> 01:13:48.890
and my copy-editor of the publisher

01:13:48.890 --> 01:13:50.627
at the University of Hawaii, emailed me,

01:13:50.627 --> 01:13:52.990
"Jin, this does not
make sense in English."

01:13:52.990 --> 01:13:55.140
And I said, "It does not
make sense in Korean either.

01:13:55.140 --> 01:13:56.351
(audience chuckling)

01:13:56.351 --> 01:13:57.850
(chuckles) What am I supposed to do?"

01:13:57.850 --> 01:14:01.570
But in a way, there's a kind
of mixture of her creativity,

01:14:01.570 --> 01:14:04.920
and then, like her use of expression,

01:14:04.920 --> 01:14:09.090
her own expression to interpret Buddhism.

01:14:09.090 --> 01:14:10.890
So this urgency

01:14:10.890 --> 01:14:15.170
and the creative use of her expressions

01:14:15.170 --> 01:14:17.680
in interpreting Buddhism,
I think that, mostly,

01:14:18.826 --> 01:14:20.270
they are distinctive for me, at least.

01:14:20.270 --> 01:14:22.783
That's a good question I
will think about more, yeah.

01:14:24.890 --> 01:14:25.890
- [Audience Member] Oh, yeah, thank you.

01:14:25.890 --> 01:14:27.980
Thanks for really a wonderful talk.

01:14:27.980 --> 01:14:30.160
I'm afraid I'm very
ignorant about this subject,

01:14:30.160 --> 01:14:33.350
so my question is a bit maybe basic.

01:14:33.350 --> 01:14:36.080
But I was just wondering to what extent,

01:14:36.080 --> 01:14:37.190
you mentioned at the beginning

01:14:37.190 --> 01:14:39.580
that there are actually more female nuns

01:14:39.580 --> 01:14:40.810
than monks in Korea,

01:14:40.810 --> 01:14:43.650
and that just stands out
in contrast to your comment

01:14:43.650 --> 01:14:45.680
about, you know, kind of
the stereotypical view

01:14:45.680 --> 01:14:47.588
of Kim Iryŏp being that
she went to a monastery

01:14:47.588 --> 01:14:49.810
and that was like the
failure of her life, right?

01:14:49.810 --> 01:14:51.210
So I'm just wondering whether

01:14:51.210 --> 01:14:54.130
either kind of alongside
Kim Iryŏp's time as a nun

01:14:54.130 --> 01:14:56.900
or sort of after that,
ideas about being a nun

01:14:56.900 --> 01:14:59.710
have changed in Korea or
whether there are other women

01:14:59.710 --> 01:15:02.110
who've taken the (indistinct)
in Korean society

01:15:02.110 --> 01:15:05.490
and whether that has
taken on new meanings,

01:15:05.490 --> 01:15:07.430
or are there, like, alternative visions

01:15:07.430 --> 01:15:09.130
of what being a nun means

01:15:09.130 --> 01:15:11.040
in kind of postwar Korean society?

01:15:11.040 --> 01:15:13.260
- Right, that's a very
good question, right?

01:15:13.260 --> 01:15:18.250
So as you know, religion had
its own function in a society,

01:15:18.250 --> 01:15:19.820
in pre-modern society.

01:15:19.820 --> 01:15:23.210
For example, monastery was
not only a religious space.

01:15:23.210 --> 01:15:26.730
It was orphanage, it was
a senior citizens' home.

01:15:26.730 --> 01:15:27.760
After you get old,

01:15:27.760 --> 01:15:28.890
if you don't have a family
to take care of you,

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:30.550
you join the monastery.

01:15:30.550 --> 01:15:34.430
If you're an orphan, you are
taken care of by the monastery.

01:15:34.430 --> 01:15:38.330
So monastery had various
different social function

01:15:38.330 --> 01:15:39.780
as they are.

01:15:39.780 --> 01:15:41.400
But in modern society,

01:15:41.400 --> 01:15:44.800
monastery gradually began
to lose that function.

01:15:44.800 --> 01:15:48.000
Also, monastery or religion

01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:49.950
had not only this kind of social function,

01:15:49.950 --> 01:15:53.590
but it had a kind of special
position in a society,

01:15:53.590 --> 01:15:57.150
because religious world,
which is sacred world,

01:15:57.150 --> 01:15:59.380
is separate from secular world.

01:15:59.380 --> 01:16:02.920
Therefore they had its own
privilege in pre-modern society.

01:16:02.920 --> 01:16:06.530
Modern society kind of get
rid of this distinction.

01:16:06.530 --> 01:16:10.590
So monastery or religious
organization needed to justify

01:16:10.590 --> 01:16:13.563
its own kind of
contribution to the society.

01:16:15.200 --> 01:16:18.193
And for women, one of the key benefits,

01:16:19.080 --> 01:16:23.600
from even Buddha's time, to
join the monastery was education

01:16:23.600 --> 01:16:28.003
and getting away from this
family household life.

01:16:29.040 --> 01:16:30.270
But gradually, gradually,

01:16:30.270 --> 01:16:34.576
when women kind of get a
opportunity to get educated,

01:16:34.576 --> 01:16:38.090
the benefit of joining the
monastery kind of diminishes.

01:16:38.090 --> 01:16:41.350
Actually, when I visited Kim Iryŏp place,

01:16:41.350 --> 01:16:43.440
one of Kim Iryŏp's disciples asked me,

01:16:43.440 --> 01:16:47.067
because I've been kind of
doing research on Kim Iryŏp,

01:16:47.067 --> 01:16:48.580
"Why didn't you join the monastery?"

01:16:48.580 --> 01:16:50.817
And then she answered her own question,

01:16:50.817 --> 01:16:52.190
"Oh, nowadays you don't have to

01:16:52.190 --> 01:16:54.280
because you can get educated anyway."

01:16:54.280 --> 01:16:55.920
So that was a main issue.

01:16:55.920 --> 01:16:58.520
And because of that, Korean society,

01:16:58.520 --> 01:17:01.010
after especially the mid-20th century,

01:17:01.010 --> 01:17:03.700
began to see those people
who joined the monastery

01:17:03.700 --> 01:17:07.530
as somebody who failed in
a secular world, right?

01:17:07.530 --> 01:17:08.960
If you are good in secular world,

01:17:08.960 --> 01:17:10.570
why do you join the monastery?

01:17:10.570 --> 01:17:12.670
And actually, that book that I translated,

01:17:14.110 --> 01:17:15.623
Kim Iryŏp's disciple,

01:17:16.590 --> 01:17:19.280
who served Kim Iryŏp until her last day,

01:17:19.280 --> 01:17:21.870
she was the one who
kind of helped Kim Iryŏp

01:17:21.870 --> 01:17:23.010
to publish that book,

01:17:23.010 --> 01:17:26.010
has a short kind of essay of her own.

01:17:26.010 --> 01:17:29.490
She says that when she was
trying to join the monastery,

01:17:29.490 --> 01:17:32.140
her family, her parents,
her brothers, her siblings,

01:17:32.140 --> 01:17:33.590
they all cried.

01:17:33.590 --> 01:17:35.780
And then she said, "I pity them

01:17:35.780 --> 01:17:37.760
because why are they crying, right,

01:17:37.760 --> 01:17:42.250
because I'm going to monastery
to find something bigger."

01:17:42.250 --> 01:17:44.990
So in a way, there is a gap, kind of gap,

01:17:44.990 --> 01:17:48.108
between the society's
perception of those people

01:17:48.108 --> 01:17:49.503
who join the monastery

01:17:49.503 --> 01:17:53.990
and those people who're
joining the monastery.

01:17:53.990 --> 01:17:56.990
And obviously, in both monks and nuns,

01:17:56.990 --> 01:18:00.240
the number of monastics
are kind of diminishing,

01:18:00.240 --> 01:18:02.050
the overall case.

01:18:02.050 --> 01:18:06.020
But I think the women's case

01:18:06.020 --> 01:18:07.610
is still that joining the monastery

01:18:07.610 --> 01:18:10.070
was to find their own kind of life

01:18:10.070 --> 01:18:13.830
outside of this family
setting or household setting.

01:18:13.830 --> 01:18:16.220
But nowadays, I don't think that people

01:18:16.220 --> 01:18:18.807
would say that explicitly, right?

01:18:18.807 --> 01:18:21.180
"You joined the monastery
because you failed," right?

01:18:21.180 --> 01:18:24.940
But that kind of the idea was there.

01:18:24.940 --> 01:18:28.050
And Robert Buswell said the
same thing about Buddhist monks

01:18:28.050 --> 01:18:30.690
in his book, "Zen Monastic
Experience," right,

01:18:30.690 --> 01:18:33.023
when he joined the monastery in 1970s.

01:18:34.060 --> 01:18:36.100
And then people, Korean people thought

01:18:36.100 --> 01:18:39.700
that there are kind of
three type of Korean monks,

01:18:39.700 --> 01:18:41.110
men who joined the monastery.

01:18:41.110 --> 01:18:43.107
First, a failure in a society.

01:18:43.107 --> 01:18:46.830
Second one was who was not happy

01:18:46.830 --> 01:18:49.470
with Western philosophy. (chuckles)

01:18:49.470 --> 01:18:52.170
So they think that the
Asian philosophy was better.

01:18:52.170 --> 01:18:53.003
What was the third one?

01:18:53.003 --> 01:18:54.500
I forgot, but there was a third one,

01:18:54.500 --> 01:18:58.130
so that kind of lazy people. (chuckles)

01:18:58.130 --> 01:19:00.730
So that was his categorization.

01:19:00.730 --> 01:19:05.730
But in a way, so there is
a mixed kind of reaction.

01:19:05.780 --> 01:19:08.573
Nowadays, I don't think
that people explicitly say

01:19:08.573 --> 01:19:10.850
that you failed because
you joined a monastery,

01:19:10.850 --> 01:19:12.480
but there was that kind of thing.

01:19:12.480 --> 01:19:14.360
But those people who joined the monastery,

01:19:14.360 --> 01:19:15.350
obviously they have

01:19:15.350 --> 01:19:16.590
the bigger goal.

01:19:21.150 --> 01:19:23.200
Well, actually, a couple of years ago

01:19:23.200 --> 01:19:25.130
when I had a conference in Taiwan,

01:19:25.130 --> 01:19:26.400
there is a Huayan school,

01:19:26.400 --> 01:19:28.860
which I regularly go for a conference.

01:19:28.860 --> 01:19:31.260
There was a young, very young Buddhist nun

01:19:31.260 --> 01:19:34.120
who just joined the monastery
after she finished college.

01:19:34.120 --> 01:19:38.470
And since my Chinese is not
good enough to communicate,

01:19:38.470 --> 01:19:40.310
she was interpreting for me,

01:19:40.310 --> 01:19:42.687
and I just asked this
stupid question, right?

01:19:42.687 --> 01:19:47.360
"So you joined the monastery.
Do you like it?" (chuckles)

01:19:47.360 --> 01:19:52.120
And then she was all smiling
and said, "I love it."

01:19:52.120 --> 01:19:53.820
And then at that moment I realized that,

01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:57.660
yeah, there are people
who really are searching

01:19:57.660 --> 01:20:00.330
for something which is bigger

01:20:00.330 --> 01:20:02.660
than we do in a secular society.

01:20:02.660 --> 01:20:06.170
And the monastery is not
totally separate nowadays

01:20:06.170 --> 01:20:07.970
and they are all (indistinct), yeah.

01:20:10.223 --> 01:20:13.197
- [Moderator] (indistinct)
question will be the last one.

01:20:14.870 --> 01:20:16.010
- [Audience Member] Thank you very much.

01:20:16.010 --> 01:20:17.610
I was wondering if you
could tell us a bit more

01:20:17.610 --> 01:20:20.860
about Kim Iryŏp's ideas about culture.

01:20:20.860 --> 01:20:21.930
You mentioned that culture

01:20:21.930 --> 01:20:24.570
is like the full expression of creativity,

01:20:24.570 --> 01:20:27.780
but at the same time, isn't
like culture and society

01:20:27.780 --> 01:20:30.680
imposing on people's ability to, like,

01:20:30.680 --> 01:20:33.480
fully find their own
creativity and express that?

01:20:33.480 --> 01:20:37.410
So how did she work that out
in her writing (indistinct)?

01:20:37.410 --> 01:20:40.210
- Why, that's a very good question.

01:20:40.210 --> 01:20:43.850
So she challenged this
patriarchal culture, right,

01:20:43.850 --> 01:20:46.030
but she used this expression.

01:20:46.030 --> 01:20:48.420
I'm yet to find where is the source

01:20:48.420 --> 01:20:51.260
of her use of this
expression, "culture," right?

01:20:51.260 --> 01:20:55.310
This "culture" also,

01:20:55.310 --> 01:20:58.006
I believe the East Asian expression,

01:20:58.006 --> 01:20:59.160
(speaking in foreign language),

01:20:59.160 --> 01:21:02.540
that is a translation from
Western language that,

01:21:02.540 --> 01:21:06.800
like many expressions, that
came from Western society

01:21:06.800 --> 01:21:09.420
and along with modernization.

01:21:09.420 --> 01:21:13.420
So Kim Iryŏp's idea of a culture

01:21:13.420 --> 01:21:17.290
is the full exercise of creativity.

01:21:17.290 --> 01:21:21.890
And when this creativity
culminates, right,

01:21:21.890 --> 01:21:23.920
at the culmination of creativity,

01:21:23.920 --> 01:21:28.920
you see certain more tangible
results, and that is culture.

01:21:28.950 --> 01:21:31.840
So for example, cultural artifacts, right?

01:21:31.840 --> 01:21:34.980
So nice building, old building at Yale,

01:21:34.980 --> 01:21:36.730
that's culture, right,

01:21:36.730 --> 01:21:39.880
in which all the spirit of the human mind

01:21:39.880 --> 01:21:41.900
is kind of condensed.

01:21:41.900 --> 01:21:43.247
But that's a good point.

01:21:43.247 --> 01:21:46.910
Kim Iryŏp didn't see culture as something

01:21:46.910 --> 01:21:51.910
which can also impose
pressure from outside.

01:21:52.560 --> 01:21:56.990
But there has been some discussion

01:21:56.990 --> 01:21:58.620
about this different way

01:21:58.620 --> 01:22:00.950
of taking this expression, "culture."

01:22:00.950 --> 01:22:04.750
So Western, in the European
expression, culture,

01:22:04.750 --> 01:22:07.690
English "culture" came from
French "cultivate," right?

01:22:07.690 --> 01:22:10.940
So cultivate, like cultivating crop,

01:22:10.940 --> 01:22:13.480
so that's why we say "cultivation."

01:22:13.480 --> 01:22:17.160
Whereas East Asian tradition

01:22:17.160 --> 01:22:19.370
didn't get that one.

01:22:19.370 --> 01:22:22.580
But (speaking in Chinese) is writing,

01:22:22.580 --> 01:22:25.158
but the second character,
Chinese character,

01:22:25.158 --> 01:22:27.260
(speaking in Chinese),
(speaking in Korean) in Korean,

01:22:27.260 --> 01:22:29.760
then means transformation.

01:22:29.760 --> 01:22:33.340
And there, I think, we
can find a similar idea

01:22:33.340 --> 01:22:36.960
of cultivation in English,
in the European language,

01:22:36.960 --> 01:22:39.670
and then transformation.

01:22:39.670 --> 01:22:43.490
And I think that's part
of Kim Iryŏp's idea

01:22:43.490 --> 01:22:48.170
about transforming yourself
through creative activity.

01:22:49.770 --> 01:22:51.530
But I think that your
question's really valid

01:22:51.530 --> 01:22:52.920
in asking that.

01:22:52.920 --> 01:22:56.210
While she resisted patriarchal culture,

01:22:56.210 --> 01:22:58.250
but she didn't call that as a culture.

01:22:58.250 --> 01:22:59.890
And I think it also,

01:22:59.890 --> 01:23:04.080
this requires a little
bit historical survey

01:23:04.080 --> 01:23:06.100
of the evolution of the concept,

01:23:06.100 --> 01:23:08.250
because this expression,
"culture," itself,

01:23:09.690 --> 01:23:13.030
came to Korea, I think, just
at the end of 19th century,

01:23:13.030 --> 01:23:16.150
and by the time of Kim Iryŏp's
use of that expression,

01:23:16.150 --> 01:23:19.570
what would be the dominant
concept of culture, right?

01:23:19.570 --> 01:23:21.488
So, but yeah.

01:23:21.488 --> 01:23:23.395
- [Moderator] Thank you so
much for your wonderful talk.

01:23:23.395 --> 01:23:24.228
- Thank you.

01:23:24.228 --> 01:23:29.228
Thank you very much.
(audience applauding)

